export const BRIEF_TEXT: string = "<<<FILE knowledge/brief/company.md>>>\n# QuotesDone — company brief for the customer-facing agents\n\nOwner: **ceo** seat (company knowledge). Compiled 12 Oct 2026 by Claude on the founder's subscription\n(founder rulings 1195, 1196, 1198). Authored, not generated by any agent through OpenRouter.\n\nThis brief is given to you in full. Answer from it by reasoning, like a senior colleague who knows the\ncompany, not like a search box: one direct, exact answer in the customer's words. If the brief does not\ncover something, say you will check, and never invent a figure, date, clause or rule.\n\n**Source keys** (every fact below carries one; the cited text governs if anything here differs):\n- `CT§n` Customer Terms of Service **v10**, last updated 6 Oct 2026 (quotesdone.com/terms)\n- `SA§n` Supplier Agreement **v15**, last updated 6 Oct 2026 (quotesdone.com/supplier-agreement)\n- `PP§n` Privacy Policy v1.6, 6 Oct 2026 · `DIS§n` Disclaimer v1.3 · `CPL` Complaints & Disputes v1.4\n- `HWV` How we verify suppliers page · `FAQ` the public FAQ (quotesdone.com/faq) · `CON` Contact page\n- `CF` src/content/company_facts.json v2026-10-06.1 (the one source of truth for every figure)\n- `FI n` the founder's ruling n in public.founder_instruction\n\nIn Arabic and English the documents are bilingual; **the Arabic text prevails**, including in court (CT§22, SA§22).\n\n---\n\n## 1. What QuotesDone is\n\n- A UAE B2B **anonymous quotation marketplace** based in Dubai. A customer describes a job (text, voice,\n  photo or drawing); the platform turns it into a priceable brief and matches it to vetted suppliers, who\n  send **anonymised** quotes. The customer compares them and pays a **3% reveal fee** on the one they choose\n  to unlock that supplier's identity and contact details. (FAQ \"What is QuotesDone?\"; CT§2)\n- Operator: **QuotesDone Technologies**, a Sole Establishment licensed by the Dubai Department of Economy and\n  Tourism (DET), Trader License **No. 1633275**, Dubai, UAE. (CT preamble, CT§20)\n- QuotesDone is a **marketplace and facilitator only**: not a contractor, supplier, agent or guarantor, and not\n  a party to the contract for the works. (CT§2, CT§8, SA§5)\n- Coverage: all seven emirates. Saudi Arabia is planned, **not live**. (FAQ \"What countries…\")\n- Trades: construction and built-environment services — fit-out and interiors, MEP (electrical, fire, HVAC,\n  plumbing), aluminium and glass, joinery and furniture, flooring and tiling, gypsum, painting,\n  waterproofing, landscaping and pools, signage, CCTV/security, smart home, solar, facilities, cleaning, pest\n  control, movers, AC maintenance and more; new trades are added as suppliers register them.\n  (FAQ \"What industries…\") Food & beverage is not a category. (src/content/categories.json `_source`)\n- No advertising and no paid ranking: nobody can pay to rank higher. (FAQ \"Can I advertise?\", \"How are suppliers ranked?\")\n\n## 2. How it works for a customer, step by step\n\n1. **Sign up.** Requests are made only after sign-up: OTP-verified mobile and email, name, date of birth\n   (under 18 is rejected), and the terms checkbox. (FI 51) WhatsApp, if a different number, is confirmed by\n   OTP inside the sign-up. (FI 391, FI 328) The platform is for users aged 18+. (PP§11)\n2. **Describe the job** by text, voice, photo, drawing, BOQ or short video. The engine asks a few\n   tap-to-answer questions to fill gaps; the request goes out only when it is complete enough to price.\n   (FAQ \"What happens after I submit…\", \"What happens if information is missing?\")\n3. **Anonymity.** Suppliers never see the customer's name, phone, email, company or exact address — only the\n   job and general area/emirate. A drawing is shared only after the customer's details on it are covered;\n   a drawing that cannot be covered is not shared. (FAQ \"Is my identity hidden?\")\n4. **Matching.** Only suppliers who can genuinely do the job, from anywhere in the UAE; usually a handful,\n   capped. \"A wrong match is worse than no match.\" Customers cannot pick or invite their own suppliers.\n   (FAQ \"How many suppliers…\", \"Can I invite my own supplier?\")\n5. **Quotes arrive** — realistically **3–5 working days**, notified as each arrives. Never promise a fixed time.\n   (FAQ \"How long does the process take?\")\n6. **Compare.** Side by side as Supplier A, B, C, normalised to the same brief: price, line items, inclusions\n   and exclusions, lead time, validity, warranty, payment terms, rating, verification status and a\n   price-confidence band. The strongest three are levelled into a **comparison report**, and **every other\n   quote is listed too — the customer can choose any of them.** (FAQ \"How are suppliers ranked?\"; FI 51)\n7. **Reveal.** The customer pays the 3% reveal fee (card or Apple Pay, via Stripe) and that one supplier's\n   identity and contact details unlock; the supplier is notified to respond. (CT§4; FAQ \"What happens after selecting…\")\n8. **Deal directly.** Price, scope and timing are then agreed between customer and supplier; QuotesDone is\n   not a party and holds no money for the job (no escrow). (CT§8, CT§15)\n\nOther customer rules (FAQ): edit an inquiry within **24 hours**; after that, create a new one. Cancel any\ntime before reveal at no cost. No quote revisions, no reopen. Declining every quote costs nothing. Budget\nis optional; the engine never invents a price or a delivery date and is brand-neutral (no approved-brand\nor approved-manufacturer list; products must meet the UAE approvals for the job).\n\n## 3. How it works for a supplier, step by step\n\n1. **Register** on the supplier portal: claim or create the company listing. (FAQ \"How do I register?\")\n2. **The mandatory six** must be complete before any match: business name (licensed legal name), mobile\n   (OTP-verified), email (verified), valid UAE trade licence, company profile (trades and emirates), signed\n   Supplier Agreement. (SA§13; HWV)\n3. **Verification** within **3 working days** of complete documents: licence number, issuing authority and\n   expiry date are checked. No supplier is approved automatically. (SA§13; HWV)\n4. **Receive matched RFQs** anonymised; ignoring an invitation carries no penalty. (FAQ \"Can I decline invitations?\")\n5. **Quote** itemised: scope, line items with brand/model and specs, quantities, unit prices, materials,\n   lead time, validity, warranty, payment terms, exclusions. A quote below the quality bar is held as a\n   draft with coaching. Submitted quotes are final (no revision); a quote may be withdrawn before reveal.\n   (FAQ \"What information is mandatory?\", \"Can I revise quotations?\", \"Can I withdraw…\")\n6. **Validity.** A quotation is valid **30 days** from submission unless refreshed or withdrawn before\n   reveal; the supplier is bound by a quotation revealed within validity. (SA§12)\n7. **Win.** The supplier is notified the moment a customer pays to reveal it, and must **begin responding\n   within 5 working days**. (SA§11, SA§33)\n8. Every supplier covers the whole UAE and may cover several trades. (FAQ \"Can I operate in multiple Emirates?\")\n9. Supplier contacts must never share or solicit phone, email, links, WhatsApp or social handles during\n   anonymised stages. (SA§10)\n\n## 4. Fees — the exact figures\n\n| Who | What | Figure | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Customer | Posting, receiving and comparing quotes | Free | CT§4 |\n| Customer | **Reveal fee** | **3%** of the selected quote's value, **no cap**, the whole price of a reveal | CT§4; CF `reveal_fee_pct` |\n| Customer | Card fee / surcharge | **None.** QuotesDone bears Stripe's charges itself | CT§4; FI 1087 |\n| Customer | VAT | None — QuotesDone is not VAT-registered; it will notify before any VAT ever applies | CT§4 |\n| Supplier | Registration, **first 1,000 businesses** | **Free, permanently** (lifetime) | SA§6; CF `supplier_free_count`; FI 856, FI 990 |\n| Supplier | From the 1,001st registration | **AED 1,000 per year**, annually in advance; 30 days' notice and re-acceptance before introducing or varying it | SA§6; CF `subscription_fee_aed` |\n| Supplier | Commission on won work | **None** — not on quote value, invoice or repeat work | SA§7 |\n| Supplier | Overdue amounts | 12% p.a. simple interest (or the lawful maximum if lower) | SA§31 |\n\n- Why no card fee: Cabinet Resolution 66/2023 (Consumer Protection executive regulation) forbids adding an\n  amount for paying by card. (FI 1087; CF `reveal_fee_pct` basis)\n- The free-1,000 offer has **no end date**. An earlier note (\"free until 31 July 2027\", FI 634) was superseded\n  by FI 856: first 1,000 free permanently, AED 1,000/yr from the 1,001st. Never quote a date.\n- Payment is by card or Apple Pay through Stripe; an invoice for the reveal fee is generated automatically,\n  issued without VAT and without a TRN. (CT§15; FAQ \"How do invoices work?\", \"How are VAT invoices generated?\")\n\n## 5. Refunds, the silent-supplier ladder, and who decides\n\n- A reveal is performed immediately on payment; the fee is then earned and **not automatically\n  refundable; there is no self-service refund.** (CT§5)\n- **The one refundable case: the revealed supplier does not respond.** The clock runs from the customer's\n  payment without the customer doing anything: day 3 (working) QuotesDone chases the supplier, copying the\n  customer; day 5 the case goes to **QuotesDone's CEO** for a final call; if the supplier still does not\n  respond, **everything the customer paid is refunded** — QuotesDone deducts nothing. Banks take **7–14\n  working days** to show it. Working days are Mon–Fri excluding UAE public holidays. (CT§5)\n- **Every refund is decided by QuotesDone's CEO**, recorded against a refund case reference quoted to the\n  customer. None is automatic. (CT§5; FI 1, FI 89)\n- No refund where the supplier responded (by any reasonable means, incl. WhatsApp or phone), the customer\n  already dealt with the supplier off-platform, the same supplier was revealed before, the customer dislikes\n  the price or terms, or simply changed their mind. Non-waivable consumer rights are preserved. (CT§5, CT§11)\n- The customer may copy **supplier@quotesdone.com** when writing to the supplier to put the attempt on record. (CT§5)\n- **The non-responding supplier then pays:** (a) the exact amount refunded (the 3% fee), (b) the processor\n  fee QuotesDone could not recover, (c) an **AED 500** administrative fee, and (d) **12% p.a.** simple\n  interest (or the lawful maximum if lower) from the due date — payable within 7 days of notice; the supplier\n  is not matched until settled. (SA§33; CF `nonresponse_admin_fee_aed`, `late_interest_pct_pa`)\n  Note: older rulings (FI 90, FI 179) said AED 250 and 9%; the signed v15 figures above replace them.\n- **Disputes between a customer and a supplier** are theirs to resolve; QuotesDone is not a party (CT§8(f)).\n  Complaints about the platform: management@quotesdone.com or the Platform; ticket number tracked at\n  quotesdone.com/complaints/status; acknowledged within **2 working days**, substantive response within\n  **10 working days**; escalation to Dubai DET Consumer Rights (Dubai Consumer app) or the Ministry of\n  Economy consumer hotline **800 1222**. (CT§18)\n- Chargebacks after a reveal (other than a genuinely unauthorised transaction) breach the terms; a business\n  customer also owes an AED 250 handling fee per dispute, a consumer only actual costs. (CT§15)\n\n## 6. Liability — QuotesDone is an intermediary\n\n- QuotesDone introduces and helps compare; any contract for goods, works or services is **solely between the\n  customer and the supplier**, consistent with **Articles 252 and 265 of the Commercial Transactions Law\n  (Federal Decree-Law No. 50 of 2022)**: an intermediary bears no liability for the performance of the\n  contract it brings about or for the value and quality of what is supplied. (CT§8; SA§17)\n- **QuotesDone is not liable for** (CT§8(a)–(f)): any supplier statement, licence, certificate or document,\n  **including any that is inaccurate, misleading or untrue** (i.e. a supplier lying in a quote — FI 1155, FI\n  1156); the contents of any quote (prices, specs, quantities, inclusions, exclusions, validity); the\n  supplier's performance, quality, safety, delays, defects or warranties; payments, deposits or refunds\n  between customer and supplier; injury, loss or damage caused by the supplier; any customer–supplier dispute.\n- The customer's remedy lies against the supplier; QuotesDone will, on request and where the law allows, give\n  the supplier details it holds and may act on the supplier's account. (CT§8)\n- Cap if a court still holds QuotesDone liable: the greater of the reveal fees paid in the prior 12 months\n  and **AED 1,000**. Not excluded: fraud or gross fault, harmful acts, death or injury, non-waivable consumer\n  rights. (CT§9)\n- Automated outputs (BOQs, estimates, comparisons, support answers) are for convenience, may contain errors,\n  and are **not professional, engineering, legal or financial advice**. (CT§14, CT§23(a))\n- QuotesDone does not guarantee any number, price or quality of quotations. (CT§14)\n- **Customer due diligence:** check the supplier's licence covers your specific works, plus permits,\n  insurance and references, before signing. Paying the reveal fee is **not an endorsement**. (DIS§4; CT§8)\n\n## 7. Verification — what \"verified\" means, exactly\n\n- Checked: the mandatory six, and on the licence its number, issuing authority and expiry date. An expired\n  licence holds the supplier out of matching until a renewal is uploaded. (HWV; SA§13)\n- **Not checked:** workmanship, financial standing/solvency, insurance (only an uploaded policy's expiry date\n  is tracked), job-specific permits, or whether the licence covers the specific works. (HWV; DIS§3)\n\n## 8. Anonymity and non-circumvention\n\n- Anonymity is the core of the service. Customers must not seek to identify or contact a supplier off-platform\n  before a paid reveal, nor use revealed identities to avoid the fee. A business customer who does owes the fee\n  avoided plus the higher of AED 5,000 or 3× the fee; a consumer owes only the fee avoided. (CT§6)\n- A supplier sharing or soliciting contact details during anonymised stages: AED 10,000 per incident; taking\n  an introduced customer off-platform: the higher of AED 20,000 or 5× the fee, for 24 months. (CF\n  `anonymity_breach_aed`, `circumvention_min_aed`, `circumvention_fee_multiple`, `circumvention_tail_months`; SA§10, SA§24)\n- These sums are agreed compensation under the Civil Transactions Law (Federal Decree-Law 25/2025, Art. 340);\n  a court keeps its power to reduce them. (CT§22; CF basis)\n\n## 9. Privacy and retention\n\n- Controller: QuotesDone Technologies; personal data processed under the UAE PDPL (Federal Decree-Law 45/2021).\n  Data is never sold. Rights (access, correction, deletion, objection) via management@quotesdone.com;\n  complaints may go to the UAE Data Office. (CT§10; PP§7–§11)\n- A customer's contact details go to **one** supplier only, after a paid reveal for that supplier. (PP)\n- Retention follows the 5-year tax-records rule (Cabinet Decision 74/2023, Art. 3) (PP§7; FI 1126):\n  account data — while active + **5 years** after last activity; conversations (email, WhatsApp, portal and\n  website chat) — while active + 5 years, and in any case 5 years from the end of the calendar year written;\n  requests, drawings, BOQs, quotes, comparison reports, and transaction records — at least 5 years from the\n  end of the calendar year created; supplier KYC — the relationship + 5 years.\n- **Unfinished sign-up** (email, mobile verification or terms acceptance not completed within 30 minutes):\n  **deleted in full after 30 minutes, nothing sent to it** — it is never a customer. (PP§7; FI 1181)\n- Some decisions (matching, ranking, quote checks, assessing refund requests) are automated; a person affected\n  may object and ask for human reconsideration. (PP)\n- Marketing only with consent; transactional messages (OTPs, quote notices, payment confirmations) are not\n  marketing. (CT§10)\n\n## 10. Languages\n\n- **Suppliers: always English**, on every channel (email, WhatsApp, SMS, PDF), including invitations and\n  inquiries sent to them. (FI 1008)\n- **Customers: the language they selected when creating the inquiry** (English or Arabic). (FI 1008) Reply\n  to a website visitor in the language they write in.\n- Legal documents are bilingual; Arabic prevails. (CT§22)\n\n## 11. Security and documents (short)\n\n- TLS in transit, encryption at rest; card data handled by Stripe (PCI DSS), never stored by QuotesDone.\n  Files in private storage via short-lived signed links; uploads up to about 1 GB; JPG, PNG, PDF, DWG, DXF.\n  (FAQ \"Security\", \"Documents\"; CT§15)\n- No public API, ERP or e-procurement connector today. (FAQ \"Integrations\")\n\n## 12. Contact\n\n- **management@quotesdone.com** · **+971 50 931 9355** · Mon–Fri 9am–6pm GST, excluding UAE public holidays. (CON; SA contact line)\n- info@quotesdone.com for questions about the Terms (CT§20); supplier@quotesdone.com to copy on a\n  non-response complaint (CT§5); complaints status at quotesdone.com/complaints/status (CT§18).\n- Governing law: UAE; courts of Dubai. (CT§19)\n\n## 13. How to answer (rules around the reasoning)\n\n- Answer the actual question directly, in the customer's language, with the figure and the reason.\n  Do not list five options when one answer is asked for. (FI 1196)\n- Technical questions (e.g. \"MDF vs plywood?\") — answer like an expert from your own knowledge and the\n  UAE regulations digest, then say the supplier proposes and prices the final specification. (FI 1196; FAQ \"Can the Smart Engine recommend specifications?\")\n- \"Compare these 3 quotes\" — compare like-for-like on what the price buys (scope, inclusions, exclusions,\n  quantities, specs/brands), completeness against the brief, lead time, validity, warranty and payment terms,\n  flag gaps, hidden costs and low-spec substitutions, then give a clear recommendation and what to clarify.\n  Never reveal or guess a supplier's identity before a paid reveal. (FI 1198; FAQ \"Quality policy\", \"What is included in the comparison?\")\n- Never promise a turnaround, a price, an approval or a refund. Refunds and anything that moves money or\n  changes policy are decided by QuotesDone's CEO. (CT§5; FAQ \"What can't the Smart Engine decide?\")\n- Regulations: give general guidance with the authority's name; the authority's current text governs, and the\n  supplier/consultant is responsible for code compliance and approvals. (FAQ \"What regulations apply?\")\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/building_structure_and_envelope.md>>>\n# UAE regulations: building, structure and envelope\n\nScope (QuotesDone category codes): general-building-contracting, masonry, structural-steel-fabrication, steel, demolition, scaffolding, roofing, waterproofing, tents-and-shade-structures, engineering-and-architectural-consultancies.\n\nCompiled 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest.\n\n**Agents: cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.**\n\nAll sources were accessed 2026-10-12. \"(unverified)\" means the claim could not be confirmed from an official source in this pass and must not be stated as fact. This file gives no clause numbers on purpose: none were verified.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mandatory codes and authorities, by emirate\n\n### Dubai (Dubai Municipality, DM)\n- **Dubai Building Code (DBC), 2021 edition.** Decree No. (45) of 2021 makes it the single building code for the whole emirate, special development zones included. It applies to building designs permitted after the decree took effect. The DBC sets *minimum* requirements, and a design may exceed them.\n  - Decree: https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2021/Decree%20No.%20(45)%20of%202021%20Concerning%20the%20Dubai%20Building%20Code.html\n  - DM page: https://www.dm.gov.ae/business/dubai-building-code/\n  - Code PDF (u.ae mirror): https://u.ae/-/media/Documents-2023/Dubai-Building-Code_English_2021-Edition_compressed-(1).pdf\n- **DEWA** checks DBC compliance when it reviews drawings for design approval. DEWA's electrical rules sit inside DBC Parts G and K. https://dewa.gov.ae/-/media/Files/Consultants-and-Contractors/Circulars/Dubai-Building-Code-Circular-EN.ashx\n- Whether a newer DBC edition has replaced the 2021 edition: (unverified). Check dm.gov.ae.\n\n### Dubai free zones and master developments\n- **Trakhees (PCFC):** a Trakhees circular makes the DBC mandatory for all permit applications in its jurisdiction from 30 Dec 2021. https://pcfc.ae/en/Rules%20and%20Regulations/Trakhees/Civil%20Engineering%20Department/Permits%20(Facilities)/Circulars/CEDGR-135%20Dubai%20Building%20Code%20Circular%202021.pdf\n- **Dubai Development Authority (DDA):** businesses in DDA areas apply for event permits and building modification permits (temporary tents, kiosks, enclosures) through the DDA portal. https://dda.gov.ae/-/media/1816C2A150044F79BCD8556F1C894A44.pdf\n- In a free zone, the zone's authority issues permits, not DM. The DBC still applies.\n\n### Abu Dhabi (Department of Municipalities and Transport, DMT)\n- **Abu Dhabi International Building Code** is one of a family of Abu Dhabi codes. It is based on the 2009 ICC International Codes and implemented by DMT. https://www.iccsafe.org/international-code-adoptions/united-arab-emirates/\n- The Executive Council adopted it, and it was made compulsory first for government projects. https://meconstructionnews.com/6732/implementation-of-abu-dhabi-international-building-code-to-improve-standards/amp\n- Adoption year and current edition: (unverified).\n- **OSHAD-SF** (Abu Dhabi OSH System Framework) is Abu Dhabi's workplace safety framework, version 3.1 issued March 2017. Abu Dhabi City Municipality has asked construction contractors, consultants and developers to register under it. https://meconstructionnews.com/30134/adm-calls-on-contractors-to-join-oshad-sf-to-improve-hse-standards/amp , https://dmt.gov.ae/en/adm/Environment-Healthy-Safty/About-EHS\n- The OSHAD code-of-practice number for scaffolding and work at height: (unverified). Third-party sources disagree, so do not quote a number.\n\n### Sharjah (Sharjah Municipality)\n- Sharjah Municipality's Contractors and Consultants Classification Section classifies contractors and consultants. A Contractors Evaluation and Classification committee was formed after a 2019 Executive Council resolution. https://portal.shjmun.gov.ae/en/MediaCenter/News/Pages/Contractors-Evaluation,-Classification-Committee-holds-its-first-meeting.aspx\n- Applications for RCC building permits are filed by an engineering consultancy office accredited by Sharjah Municipality, not by the contractor. Source: dubizzle guide, secondary: https://www.dubizzle.com/blog/property/apply-building-permit-sharjah/\n- Sharjah Housing-supervised residential permits need the contractor's valid licence, the contractor engineer card and the contractor contract. https://malihamun.shj.ae/en/services/issuing-a-residential-building-permit-under-the-supervision-of-sharjah-housing\n- Name or edition of a Sharjah building code: (unverified).\n\n### Other emirates (Ajman, RAK, UAQ, Fujairah)\n- Not researched in this pass. Codes and authorities: (unverified).\n\n### Federal (all emirates)\n- **Midday work break (MoHRE).** Outdoor work in direct sun is banned from 12:30 to 15:00, 15 June to 15 September. 2025 was the 21st consecutive year. The fine is AED 5,000 per worker, up to AED 50,000. Employers must provide shaded rest areas, cold water and first aid. Work that cannot technically be stopped (for example a concrete pour or asphalt) can be exempt. Reports go to 600590000. Source: news coverage of MoHRE's announcement: https://gulfnews.com/uae/uae-announces-summer-midday-break-june-15-to-september-15-1230pm-to-3pm-1.88451178 , https://en.aletihad.ae/news/uae/4489967/mohre-implements-midday-break-from-june-15-to-september-15\n  - The 2026 dates were not confirmed from a MoHRE source: (unverified). The pattern has been stable every year.\n- **Steel rebar conformity (MoIAT/ECAS).** Under a 2023 Cabinet Resolution, carbon-steel reinforcing bars made or imported in the UAE, free zones included, need an ECAS Conformity Certificate before they go on sale, and test reports must be kept for 10 years. Billets and coils used to make rebar are also covered. https://uaelegislation.gov.ae/en/legislations/2346/download\n  - Industry reporting gives a preparation period from 1 May 2025 to 1 Nov 2026: (unverified with MoIAT). https://www.steelradar.com/en/ecas-mandatory-for-rebar-in-abu-dhabi\n  - ECAS certificate valid for 1 year, renewable. Source: SGS, secondary: https://www.sgs.com/-/media/sgscorp/documents/corporate/brochures/sgs-uae-moiat-data-sheet-en-22.cdn.en.pdf\n- **Concrete blocks:** whether ECAS certification is mandatory for them was not confirmed: (unverified).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Permits and approvals\n\n- **Three separate things:** the trade licence (the company exists), contractor classification (the company may practise the activity at a grade), and the project building permit (this specific job). A licence and a classification do not replace the building permit. Source: secondary guides summarising DM practice, https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/4.-Contracting-Activities-Classfication.pdf (activity list)\n- **Building permits in Dubai** are issued by DM, or in free zones by the zone authority (Trakhees, DDA). Civil Defence approval forms part of the approval chain for life-safety items.\n- **Demolition:** DM lists \"Wrecking & Demolition Works\" (activity 451001) as its own specialised contracting activity. https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/4.-Contracting-Activities-Classfication.pdf\n  - Demolition permit documents, fees and steps: (unverified). Check DM's permit services.\n- **Tents and event structures (Dubai):** an event tent needs a special permit through the ePermits portal (Dubai Economy and Tourism). Required documents include a Civil Defence approval or NOC and a DM approval letter. DET estimates AED 5,000 to 15,000 per month. https://dubaidet.gov.ae/en/our-services/for-consumers-and-students/temporary-tent-permit\n  - Older DM rules required Civil Defence approval before the DM permit, transportable foundations, and no blocking of roads, parking or utilities. Fines reached up to AED 50,000. These are older news sources and may be outdated: https://gulfnews.com/uae/set-up-ramadan-tents-but-follow-rules-1.1353523 , https://thenationalnews.com/uae/municipalities-get-tough-over-ramadan-tents-1.551783\n- **Residential parking shades (Dubai)** are an RTA permit, not a DM permit. Installers must be RTA-approved companies, using approved colours and materials, and the shades must be removable. Source: secondary: https://www.pitstoparabia.com/en/news/residential-parking-shade-permit\n- **Sharjah maintenance permits** need a Sharjah-issued contractor licence and an owner-contractor agreement. Source: dubizzle, secondary: https://www.dubizzle.com/blog/property/property-maintenance-permit-renewal-sharjah/\n\n---\n\n## 3. Contractor and consultant licensing and classification\n\n### Contractors, Dubai\n- **Dubai Law No. 7 of 2025** puts contracting under a unified DM Contractor Register:\n  - Contracting has been a registered, classified activity since 8 Jan 2026.\n  - It covers free zones and the DIFC.\n  - Project value ceilings are tied to the contractor's classification.\n  - Subcontracting needs prior approval.\n  - Contractors licensed in Dubai for the first time start in the lowest category.\n  - Existing contractors must comply by 8 Jan 2027.\n  - Technical staff in building, construction and demolition activities need DM professional competency certificates.\n  - Source: law-firm summary, secondary: https://neolegal.ae/insights/dubai-contractor-registration-law-7-2025 , https://www.fenwickelliott.com/?p=34794\n  - Exact text and dates against the official legislation portal (dlp.dubai.gov.ae): (unverified).\n- **Grade scale:** secondary sources disagree. One describes \"G+1 to Unlimited\", another \"Grade 1 to 5\". Do not quote a grade scale: (unverified).\n- DM's activity codes: Building Contracting 452001 (a main activity with grades) and Wrecking & Demolition 451001. https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/4.-Contracting-Activities-Classfication.pdf\n\n### Engineering and architectural consultancies, Dubai\n- **Dubai Law No. 14 of 2025** replaces the 1994 local order.\n  - No one may provide or advertise engineering consultancy without a trade licence and DM registration. Registration lasts 1 year and is renewed annually.\n  - Classification is based on experience and capability, mainly the registered engineers.\n  - A branch of a UAE office needs at least 3 consecutive years of experience. A branch of a foreign office needs at least 10.\n  - Offices must not work outside their licensed scope or use unregistered engineers.\n  - Fines run up to AED 100,000, with suspension, downgrading or deregistration.\n  - Sources: https://mediaoffice.ae/en/news/2025/october/05-10/mohammed-bin-rashid , https://www.tamimi.com/news/dubai-issues-new-law-regulating-engineering-consultancy-activities-key-takeaway-points/\n  - Effective date: secondary sources say around April 2026, with 1 year for existing offices to regularise: (unverified).\n- **Society of Engineers UAE:** any role in consultant or engineer registration: (unverified). Nothing was found.\n\n### Contractors and consultants, Sharjah\n- Classified by Sharjah Municipality's Contractors and Consultants Classification Section, which offers online tests and star ratings. https://portal.shjmun.gov.ae/en/faq/pages/home.aspx\n- Grade tiers: (unverified).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Thresholds buyers ask about (verified only)\n\n| Topic | Verified figure | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Midday outdoor-work ban | 12:30 to 15:00, 15 Jun to 15 Sep (2025 cycle) | gulfnews / aletihad (above) |\n| Midday break fine | AED 5,000 per worker, max AED 50,000 | same |\n| Rebar | ECAS Conformity Certificate before sale; test reports kept 10 years | uaelegislation.gov.ae/en/legislations/2346 |\n| Dubai consultancy registration | 1 year, annual renewal; fines up to AED 100,000 | mediaoffice.ae, tamimi.com |\n| Dubai Contractor Register | in force 8 Jan 2026; existing contractors by 8 Jan 2027 | neolegal.ae (secondary) |\n| Dubai event tent permit | about AED 5,000 to 15,000 per month (DET estimate) | dubaidet.gov.ae |\n| DBC in Trakhees areas | mandatory from 30 Dec 2021 | pcfc.ae circular |\n\nNot verified, so do not quote: contractor grade value caps, demolition permit fees, scaffold inspection intervals, the roof or waterproofing warranty years required by any authority, and any DBC clause numbers.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Spec terms glossary (brief, general trade usage, not regulatory)\n\n- **G+n**: ground plus n upper floors (building height description).\n- **RCC**: reinforced cement concrete.\n- **Rebar grade (e.g. B500B)**: yield strength class of the reinforcing steel. Ask for the mill certificate and ECAS certificate.\n- **Hollow or solid block (e.g. 200 mm)**: concrete masonry unit, given by thickness. Ask for the load-bearing or non-load-bearing class.\n- **MS / GI / HDG**: mild steel / galvanised iron / hot-dip galvanised.\n- **Shop drawings**: the fabricator's detailed drawings, approved by the consultant before fabrication.\n- **Membrane (torch-on bitumen, PVC/TPO, liquid PU)**: types of waterproofing layer. Ask for the thickness, number of layers and the flood test.\n- **Flood test**: water held on a waterproofed roof or wet area (often 24 to 48 h, by trade practice) to prove it does not leak.\n- **Inverted roof**: insulation laid above the waterproofing membrane.\n- **PTFE / PVC tensile fabric**: membranes used in shade structures and tents. Ask for the fire-rating certificate (Civil Defence).\n- **Scaffold tag (green/red)**: trade practice showing whether a scaffold has been inspected and is safe or unsafe.\n- **NOC**: no-objection certificate from an authority or the master developer.\n- **Completion certificate**: the authority's sign-off at the end of a permitted project.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What a buyer should check in a quote\n\n1. **Licence and activity:** the trade licence lists the exact activity (for example demolition 451001 or building contracting 452001 in Dubai) and is valid in the emirate where the work is.\n2. **Classification fits the job:** the contractor's grade covers the project size and value. In Dubai this has been registered in the DM Contractor Register since Jan 2026.\n3. **Consultant:** works that need a permit need a registered or accredited engineering consultant. In Dubai the consultant is registered with DM. In Sharjah, an accredited office submits the RCC permit.\n4. **Who gets the permits:** the quote says who obtains the building, demolition or tent permit and the Civil Defence or NOC approvals, and who pays the fees.\n5. **Materials compliance:** for rebar, the ECAS conformity certificate and mill certificates. For other products, any certificates the consultant requires.\n6. **Code:** the design and works follow the governing code: the DBC in Dubai (Trakhees and DDA included), and the Abu Dhabi codes and OSHAD-SF in Abu Dhabi.\n7. **Safety and programme:** the programme allows for the summer midday break, and scaffolding and work at height carry a method statement and risk assessment.\n8. **Scope exclusions:** the quote lists the exclusions: authority fees, temporary works, debris removal, testing (including the flood test), and making good.\n9. **Warranty:** the waterproofing and roofing warranty period, and who gives it (applicator or manufacturer). No statutory figure was verified here.\n10. **Subcontracting:** in Dubai, subcontracting needs prior approval under the 2025 law. Ask whether the work will be subcontracted.\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/electrical_power_and_low_current.md>>>\n# UAE regulations: electrical, power and low-current trades\n\nScope (category codes): `electrical`, `generators`, `solar`, `smart-home`, `it-networking`, `av-systems`\nCompiled 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest.\n**Agents: cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.**\n\nAll sources accessed 2026-10-12. \"(unverified)\" means it was not confirmed on an official or authoritative source. Clause numbers are given only where the authority's own document names them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Who regulates what, by emirate\n\n| Emirate | Electricity authority | Wiring code | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dubai | DEWA (Dubai Electricity & Water Authority) | DEWA Regulations for Electrical Installations, 2017 Edition. It replaces the 1997 edition and is still in force alongside later DEWA circulars, including the Dubai Building Code circular | https://dewa.gov.ae/-/media/DEWA_Regulations_for_Electrical_Installations_2017_Edition.ashx ; https://dewa.gov.ae/en/builder/useful-tools/dewa-circulars ; https://dewa.gov.ae/-/media/Files/Consultants-and-Contractors/Circulars/Dubai-Building-Code-Circular-EN.ashx |\n| Abu Dhabi (incl. Al Ain) | DoE (Department of Energy) sets the rules. TAQA Distribution (formerly ADDC/AADC) licenses contractors | Electricity Wiring Regulations (EWR) 2020. In force from 1 April 2020, replacing the 2014 issue. Based on BS 7671, with appendix tables updated to BS 7671 18th edition | https://www.doe.gov.ae/-/media/Project/DOE/Department-Of-Energy/Media-Center-Publications/EWR-2020-EN-V8-B-Online.pdf ; https://www.doe.gov.ae/en/Media-Centre/News/DoE-Issues-Revised-Electricity-Wiring-Regulations |\n| Sharjah | SEWA (Sharjah Electricity, Water & Gas Authority) | Rules and Regulations for Electrical Wiring Installations, 3rd edition 2019, replacing the 1980 and 2003 editions. Found only through secondary copies (unverified on sewa.gov.ae) | https://www.scribd.com/document/397234222/Sewa (secondary) |\n| Ajman, Umm Al Quwain, Ras Al Khaimah, Fujairah | Etihad Water & Electricity (EtihadWE), which took over from FEWA. Older paperwork may still say \"FEWA\" | Wiring code edition not confirmed (unverified) | https://www.etihadwe.ae/Documents/dss/Distributed%20Solar%20systems%20Conditions%20_%20EtihadWE_Final.pdf |\n\nFederal level:\n- **MoIAT** (Ministry of Industry and Advanced Technology) runs **ECAS**, the mandatory conformity scheme for regulated products. Low-voltage electrical products, LV cables, lighting, RoHS, energy-efficiency labelling and Ex equipment all sit under it. **EQM** is MoIAT's quality mark. Sources: https://www.sgs.com/en-ae/news/2024/06/pca-2024-q2-navigating-uae-product-conformity-understanding-ecas-and-eqm-certification ; https://www.tuvsud.com/en-ae/services/product-certification/moiat-ecas-product-certification-services-in-uae (both are notified-body pages, not MoIAT itself).\n- **TDRA** (Telecommunications and Digital Government Regulatory Authority, formerly TRA) handles type approval of telecom and radio equipment. Source: https://www.tuv.com/market-access-services/en/certification-filter/united-arab-emirates-telecommunication-apparatus-(tdra)-approval.html (secondary). The TDRA site itself was not reached.\n\n## 2. Contractor and consultant approvals\n\n**Dubai (DEWA)**\n- The 2017 Regulations contain a section titled \"DEWA enrolment for consultants and electrical contractors\". DEWA also publishes separate circulars on enrolment/registration of consultants and contractors and on their authorised engineers. Electrical work that connects to DEWA must be designed and installed by DEWA-enrolled firms. https://dewa.gov.ae/en/builder/useful-tools/dewa-circulars\n- Private 11 kV substations need a firm on DEWA's separate approved list for private 11 kV substation contractors. https://www.dewa.gov.ae/-/media/Files/PVT/Mandatory-requirements-for-enlisting-as-DEWA-approved-Private-11KV-Substation-Contractors.ashx\n- Claims of contractor \"grades\" or a \"DEWA competency assessment\" come from exam-prep sites (unverified).\n\n**Abu Dhabi (TAQA Distribution under the DoE)**\n- TAQA Distribution assesses and licenses electrical contractors for installation work in buildings across the emirate, acting under DoE authority. It publishes a list of certified contractors and a drawing-submission checklist. https://addc.ae/en-US/distribution/Pages/Contractors.aspx\n- The approval process, Revision 2 dated 1 April 2023 and approved by the DoE, implements Regulation 3.4 of the EWR. It requires consultants who design installations, and Solar PV integrators, to hold a Competency Certificate from the Distribution Company. https://www.addc.ae/en-US/distribution/Documents/Process%20For%20Approval%20of%20Licened%20Contractors.pdf\n- That licensing covers LV work only. HV work is referred to the distribution company (stated in the 2015 process; current position unverified).\n\n**Sharjah (SEWA)**\n- Projects are submitted to SEWA and approved before site work starts, and supply is not given unless the rules are fully met. New connections, load increases and additional meters need the trade licence of a SEWA-approved electrical contractor. Sources are secondary: https://www.bayut.com/mybayut/sewa-connection-sharjah . The SEWA registration process and fees were not found (unverified).\n\n**Northern Emirates (EtihadWE)**\n- Distributed solar must be done by an \"Approved Contractor\", meaning a system integrator authorised by EtihadWE (definition in the official Connection Conditions). The process for general electrical contractor approval was not found (unverified).\n\n## 3. Solar PV and net metering\n\n**Dubai: Shams Dubai (DEWA)**\n- Residential and commercial PV owners may export surplus to the grid under net metering, with the surplus offset at the same price as consumption. https://www.dewa.gov.ae/en/about-us/media-publications/latest-news/2015/11/dewas-shams-dubai-initiative-to-generate-long-lasting-renewable-energy\n- **Only firms enrolled with DEWA as Solar PV consultants or contractors may submit applications.** Their staff must pass DEWA's PV expert training and certification. DEWA publishes the enrolled list, which was updated 18 Jul 2025 at the time of search, and disclaims responsibility for listed firms' work. https://www.dewa.gov.ae/-/media/Files/Consultants-and-Contractors/Enrolled-Solar-PV-Contractors-and-Consultants.ashx\n- DEWA certified 1,115 specialists and enrolled 111 companies for Shams Dubai in 2024. https://www.dewa.gov.ae/en/about-us/media-publications/latest-news/2025/06/dewa-certified\n- Sequence: Solar NOC, then design approval, then installation, then DEWA inspection and connection. This order is reported by secondary sources (partly unverified).\n- Reports that DEWA restricted net metering for some segments: https://www.enerdata.net/publications/daily-energy-news/dubai-uae-restricts-net-metering-scheme-solar-pv.html (secondary; current scope unverified, so check with DEWA).\n\n**Abu Dhabi**\n- The Small-Scale Solar PV Energy Netting Regulation has been in effect since January 2017. It covers systems up to 5 MW per premises connected to the LV network. Surplus kWh are credited and carried forward rather than paid in cash. Projects need municipal permits and regulator approval before connection. https://pv-magazine.com/2017/06/21/abu-dhabi-releases-safety-standards-for-pv-under-net-metering ; https://pv-tech.org/abu-dhabi-establishes-net-metering-for-small-scale-solar (trade press; primary text not retrieved, so the exact eligibility is unverified)\n- PV integrators need a TAQA Distribution Competency Certificate (see section 2).\n\n**Northern Emirates (EtihadWE Distributed Solar Systems)**\n- The Connection Conditions are issued under Federal Decree-Law No. 17 of 2022. Phase 1 covers residential, industrial and agricultural customers. Monthly excess export is credited for use within the same year. https://www.etihadwe.ae/Documents/dss/Distributed%20Solar%20systems%20Conditions%20_%20EtihadWE_Final.pdf\n- Vendor sources claim a 20 MW aggregate cap and that credits are forfeited at 31 December (unverified).\n\n**Sharjah**\n- No official SEWA solar or net-metering text was found (unverified).\n\n## 4. EV chargers\n\n- **Dubai:** Supreme Council of Energy Directive No. 1 of 2017 requires DEWA approval before any public or private body installs, operates or maintains an EV charging station. DEWA coordinates with Dubai Municipality and RTA on standards. https://gulfnews.com/uae/government/new-rules-for-electric-car-chargers-1.1986003 ; https://rise.esmap.org/data/files/library/united-arab-emirates/Energy%20Efficiency/UAE_installation%20of%20electric%20vehicle%20charging%20stations%202017%20in%20Dubai.pdf\n- Permit fees (AED 5,000-20,000) and the claim that DC chargers above 22 kW need a dedicated supply application come from a commercial blog (unverified).\n- Other emirates: no official EV-charger approval text was found (unverified). Assume a utility load application is needed.\n\n## 5. Generators\n\n- No emirate-level generator-specific code was retrieved. Common practice is that a standby generator feeding a building installation needs a changeover or interlock that prevents back-feed into the utility network, approved through the utility's drawing submission. This is a general principle of the BS 7671-based codes (unverified for the specific clause).\n- Diesel storage, exhaust and noise are typically governed by Civil Defence and municipality rules (unverified, not researched here).\n\n## 6. Product conformity buyers ask about\n\n- **ECAS Certificate of Conformity (CoC)** is required before regulated products are imported or sold. CoCs are valid for **1 year** with annual renewal. **EQM** certificates are valid for **3 years**. For LV equipment, IEC CB-scheme test reports can support the file. English and Arabic manuals and rating labels are part of the file. Source: SGS notified-body pages above.\n- **TDRA type approval** applies to equipment that connects to a public network or has an intentional radio transmitter: Wi-Fi access points and routers, Bluetooth and Zigbee/Z-Wave hubs, DECT, RFID, GPS trackers. It is held by the registered manufacturer, importer or distributor before sale. Secondary sources say the certificate is valid for 3 years, the dealer registration for 5 years, and the TDRA mark goes on the packaging (unverified on tdra.gov.ae). Source: TÜV pages above.\n- Smart-home and AV kit that is wireless (smart switches, Wi-Fi cameras, wireless mics, soundbars with Bluetooth) therefore typically needs **both** ECAS (as an electrical product) **and** TDRA approval (as radio equipment). Whether a specific product needs both is unverified per product.\n\n## 7. IT networking and structured cabling\n\n- TDRA regulates telecom equipment and the licensed operators (e&, du). Building telecom-readiness specifications, such as in-building fibre and telecom room requirements, are set with the operators and building authorities. The specific current document was not retrieved (unverified).\n- No UAE-mandated structured-cabling standard was confirmed. Specs commonly cite ISO/IEC 11801 or TIA-568 categories (Cat6/Cat6A, OM3/OM4, OS2). This is industry practice, not a verified UAE mandate.\n\n## 8. Glossary (brief)\n\n- **NOC**: No Objection Certificate from an authority. It must be issued before design approval or work starts.\n- **DB / SMDB / MDB**: distribution board / sub-main DB / main DB.\n- **RCD / RCBO / MCB / MCCB / ACB**: residual-current device / RCD + overcurrent breaker / miniature, moulded-case and air circuit breakers.\n- **ATS / AMF**: automatic transfer switch / automatic mains failure panel (generator changeover).\n- **kVA vs kW**: apparent vs real power. Generators are rated in kVA at a stated power factor (usually 0.8). Ask for prime vs standby rating.\n- **kWp**: peak DC capacity of a PV array. **Inverter kW (AC)** is often lower than kWp.\n- **Net metering / energy netting**: exports offset imports on the bill. Credits are not cash (see section 3 per emirate).\n- **ECAS CoC / EQM**: MoIAT product conformity certificate / quality mark.\n- **TDRA TA**: TDRA type approval for radio and telecom devices.\n- **Cat6A, OM4, OS2**: copper and fibre cable grades. **PoE**: power over Ethernet.\n- **As-built drawings**: final drawings submitted for utility inspection and handover.\n\n## 9. What a buyer should check in a quote\n\n1. The contractor's **enrolment or licence with the right utility** for the emirate: DEWA (Dubai), TAQA Distribution (Abu Dhabi), SEWA (Sharjah), EtihadWE (Northern Emirates). For solar in Dubai, check the firm is on DEWA's **Enrolled Solar PV Contractors and Consultants** list. In Abu Dhabi, check for a Competency Certificate.\n2. **Who obtains the approvals**: NOC, drawing or design approval, inspection, meter or connection. Check these steps and their fees are in the price.\n3. The **code the design follows**, named by edition: DEWA 2017 Regulations, DoE EWR 2020 or SEWA 2019.\n4. **ECAS/EQM certificates** for breakers, cables, fittings, luminaires and inverters, and **TDRA approval** for any wireless or networked device.\n5. **Brand and model** for every major item, such as panels, inverter, generator set, switchgear and access points. Avoid \"or equivalent\" with no approval mechanism.\n6. **Ratings** stated: generator kVA prime/standby, PV kWp and inverter kW, breaker kA breaking capacity, cable sizes, and the cable category.\n7. **Testing and handover**: test certificates, as-built drawings, warranties (panel and inverter warranties are separate), and O&M manuals.\n8. **Exclusions**: civil works, utility fees, load-increase charges, scaffolding, and roof or structural certification.\n\n---\nGaps to close next pass (all marked unverified above): the SEWA contractor registration process and any solar scheme; the EtihadWE wiring code edition; the official TDRA type-approval page; EV-charger rules outside Dubai; generator-specific clauses; the in-building telecom specifications.\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/facade_glazing_metal_lifts_signage.md>>>\n# UAE regulations: facades, glazing, metal openings, lifts and signage\n\n**Scope (QuotesDone trade categories):** `aluminium` (windows, doors, curtain wall), `glass` (glazing, safety glass), `garage-doors` (shutters), `elevators` (lifts, escalators), `signage` (outdoor advertising and signage permits), `acrylic`.\n\nCompiled 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest.\n\n**Agents: cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.**\n\nHow to read this file:\n- Every item carries its source and an access date of 2026-10-12.\n- \"(unverified)\" means the only source found was secondary (news, consultant, manufacturer, or a commercial template), or the sources disagree. Never present an unverified item to a buyer as a rule. Say \"commonly reported\" and point the buyer to the authority.\n- No clause numbers are given unless a source stated them. Do not invent any.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Authorities and codes by subject\n\n| Subject | Dubai | Abu Dhabi | All emirates |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Facade and cladding fire safety | Dubai Civil Defence (DCD); Emirates Safety Laboratory (ESL) tests and certifies | Abu Dhabi Civil Defence Authority (ADCD) | UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice (2018 edition), which has a section on facade systems |\n| Envelope and energy (glazing performance) | Dubai Municipality (DM): Dubai Building Code; Al Sa'fat green building rating | (unverified for this file) | — |\n| Glass certification | DM Dubai Central Laboratory: \"Certification of Glazed Glass as per related sections of Al Sa'fat and Dubai Building Code\" | — | — |\n| Product conformity (regulated products) | — | — | Ministry of Industry and Advanced Technology (MoIAT): ECAS / EQM certificates |\n| Lifts and escalators | DM Health and Safety: examination and certification of lifts and lifting equipment | Civil Defence enforces maintenance contracts; ADQCC handles lifting-equipment inspection competency (unverified for passenger lifts) | — |\n| Outdoor signs and advertising | Decree No. 6 of 2020; DM and RTA, or the free-zone or master-developer authority | (not researched) | — |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Facades and cladding (aluminium, curtain wall, ACP, acrylic or composite panels on a facade)\n\n1. **The 2018 UAE Fire and Life Safety Code has a dedicated section on facade system design.** A law-firm summary places it in Chapter 1, Section 4. That location comes from a secondary source, so quote it only as \"reported\". Sources: https://bsalaw.com/?p=4119 ; https://wfmmedia.com/gcc-codes-facade-fire-key-methodogies/ (accessed 2026-10-12).\n2. **Practitioner \"ten-point\" approach to Civil Defence approval (unverified as official wording).** The reported points are:\n   - Test the core material in exposed form.\n   - Test the panel as a product.\n   - Test the full wall assembly, or use a listed system.\n   - Install cavity fire barriers at every slab.\n   - Use facade contractors and fabricators approved and licensed by Civil Defence.\n   - Have the facade inspected during installation and certified by a facade fire expert or a Civil Defence-approved house of expertise.\n\n   Source: https://wfmmedia.com/gcc-codes-facade-fire-key-methodogies/ (accessed 2026-10-12).\n3. **Dubai: ESL certificate of conformity.** DCD set up the Emirates Safety Laboratory. Reports say cladding materials and facade systems need an ESL certificate of conformity, and that tests done at other accredited labs must be assessed by ESL before it issues the certificate. Without the certificate, approvals for construction work are not issued. The deadline reported in 2023 was \"by October\", and whether it took effect as described is unverified. Sources: https://www.thenationalnews.com/uae/2023/06/06/cladding-fire-dubai-skyscraper/ ; https://gulfnews.com/uae/dubai-announces-new-fire-safety-standards-1.95253431 ; https://beale-law.com/?p=11420 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n4. **Older Dubai rule: Dubai Central Laboratory conformity certificate for all facade material (unverified as current).** It may have been superseded by the ESL route. Source: https://gulfnews.com/amp/story/uae%2Fauthorities-tighten-rules-on-building-materials-1.1107359 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n5. **A DM circular allowed only fire-retardant panels on residential and office tower facades (reported).** Source: as in item 3 (Gulf News 2019 to 2023 coverage), accessed 2026-10-12.\n6. **Manufacturer approval claims are not a register.** For example, Sto announced DCD and ADCD \"approved status\" for its facade systems. Verify any claimed approval with the authority. Source: https://sto.com/en/news-detail_16320.html (accessed 2026-10-12).\n\n## 3. Glazing performance and glass\n\n1. **Al Sa'fat applies to all new buildings in Dubai.** Silver Sa'fa is the minimum, and Gold and Platinum are enhanced tiers. The current text is the 2nd Edition (January 2023) (unverified edition date; secondary source). Source: https://www.propertyfinder.ae/blog/dubai-green-building-regulations/ (accessed 2026-10-12).\n2. **Numeric glazing limits in Dubai: UNVERIFIED, and the sources conflict.** Do not quote these numbers to buyers as code. Ask the consultant for the project's compliance sheet.\n   - One industry source: U-value below 2.1, shading coefficient (SC) at or below 0.4, light transmittance at or above 40%, with stricter limits above 40% glazing area.\n   - Another industry source: SC 0.25 and U-value 1.4 for most glazing, with SC 0.4 at 40% glazing or less and 0.25 at 60% or more.\n\n   Sources: https://www.arabianbusiness.com/industries/construction/passing-fa-ade-381529 ; https://wfmmedia.com/building-glass-facades/ (accessed 2026-10-12).\n3. **DM glass certification document exists.** DM-DCLD-RD-DP32-5103 (Rev. 11, July 2024), \"Certification of Glazed Glass as per Related sections of Al Sa'fat and Dubai Building Code\". Only the cover was read, so its content is unverified. Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/DM-DCLD-RD-DP32-5103-IC.pdf (accessed 2026-10-12).\n4. **The World Bank profile lists the Dubai energy code as setting a maximum SHGC as a mandatory requirement for residential buildings.** No figure is given. Source: https://www.worldbank.org/en/building-green/explorecountries/united-arab-emirates (accessed 2026-10-12).\n5. **Safety glass (where toughened or laminated glass is mandatory): no source found. (unverified)** Refer buyers to the Dubai Building Code text or their consultant.\n\n## 4. Product conformity: MoIAT ECAS / EQM (all emirates)\n\n1. **Regulated products must hold an ECAS or EQM certificate to be sold in the UAE, whether imported or made locally.** Requirements are a valid trade licence and a test report from an accredited laboratory. Sources: https://www.moiat.gov.ae/en/services/issue-conformity-certificates-for-regulated-products ; https://www.sgs.com/en/news/2024/06/pca-2024-q2-navigating-uae-product-conformity-understanding-ecas-and-eqm-certification (accessed 2026-10-12).\n2. **An ECAS certificate is valid for one year with annual renewal.** This is per SGS, a MoIAT notified body (secondary source). Source: the SGS URL above (accessed 2026-10-12).\n3. **Glass products appear in MoIAT's public product-conformity register.** Whether a specific glass or aluminium product is a regulated product is unverified. Check the register or the relevant technical regulation. Source: https://moiat.gov.ae/en/open-data/product-conformity?page=1554 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n\n## 5. Lifts and escalators (elevators)\n\n1. **Dubai: every lift, escalator, moving walkway and conveyor needs a certificate of examination and compliance.** It is issued by a company accredited by DM's accreditation centre and renewed periodically (2017 statement by a DM official). Source: https://www.pressreader.com/uae/khaleej-times/20170914/281698319912316 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n2. **DM publishes guideline DM-HSD-GU48-ECLA2, \"Technical Guidelines for Examination and Certification of Cranes, Hoists, Lifts and Other Lifting Equipment\" (V5).** The PDF could not be parsed for this file, so the inspection interval is unverified. Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/DM-HSD-GU48-ECLA2_Technical-Guidelines-for-Examination-and-Certification-of-Cranes-Hoists-Lifts-and-Other-Lifting-Equipment_V5.pdf (accessed 2026-10-12).\n3. **Reported Dubai practice (unverified; consultancy source):**\n   - No lift may operate without a valid examination certificate.\n   - Installation and maintenance are done by DM-registered companies.\n   - Every lift is under a maintenance contract.\n   - Periodic third-party inspection is done by an EIAC-accredited body independent of the installer.\n\n   Source: https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/elevator-lift-company-dubai (accessed 2026-10-12).\n4. **Dubai escalator circular: buildings and malls must fit protective covers and anti-climb protection at escalators.** Its basis is Local Order No. 11 of 2003. Source: https://www.pressreader.com/uae/khaleej-times/20170324/281694024605501 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n5. **Abu Dhabi: AED 5,000 fine where a lift has no maintenance contract with an accredited maintenance entity.** It applies to existing buildings and buildings under construction, except independent private houses. The basis is Cabinet Resolution No. 24 of 2012 on Civil Defence services. Source: https://www.khaleejtimes.com/uae/abu-dhabi-5000-fine-building-owners-no-elevator-maintenance-contract (accessed 2026-10-12).\n6. **Abu Dhabi periodic inspection interval and certificate: not found. (unverified)** ADS 22/2017 on lifting-equipment inspection is reported, but its coverage of passenger lifts is unverified. Source: https://leeaint.com/leea-news/news-leea-and-adqcc-continue-consultations-for-proficiency-testing-of-lifting-equipment-inspectors (accessed 2026-10-12).\n\n## 6. Signage and outdoor advertising (signage, acrylic signs)\n\n1. **Dubai: Decree No. 6 of 2020 governs advertising.** It repealed Local Order No. 6 of 1986 and requires DM to issue a manual. Source: https://www.khaleejtimes.com/uae/dubai-to-impose-curbs-on-outdoor-advertising-2 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n2. **The permitting body depends on location:**\n   - DM\n   - RTA\n   - Department of Economy (formerly DED)\n   - the free-zone or private-development authority\n   - Dubai Civil Aviation Authority\n   - Dubai Maritime City Authority\n\n   Source: https://campaignme.com/marking-your-outdoor-territory-by-al-tamimis-fiona-robertson/ (accessed 2026-10-12).\n3. **Each sign, including a shopfront facade sign, needs its own prior permit covering location, structure and content. A trade licence alone does not cover it (unverified; consultancy source).** The same source reports a single platform, \"Mada Media\", issuing permits for RTA and DM (unverified). Source: https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/signage-advertising-company-setup-dubai (accessed 2026-10-12).\n4. **Prohibited locations include:**\n   - historic, government and residential buildings\n   - traffic lights and signs\n   - trees\n   - places of worship\n   - military and restricted areas\n\n   Source: https://gulfnews.com/business/dubai-residential-buildings-cannot-feature-ads-any-longer-1.69943225 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n5. **When the permit expires, the advertiser must remove the sign and restore the site.** Source: as in item 2 (accessed 2026-10-12).\n6. **Size limits: the only figure found (10 m x 30 m hoardings) was a 2010 draft. (unverified; do not quote)**\n\n## 7. Short glossary of spec terms\n\n- **ACP:** aluminium composite panel. The core type (polyethylene vs fire-retardant or mineral core) is what Civil Defence approval turns on.\n- **Curtain wall (stick or unitised):** a non-structural aluminium-and-glass facade hung from the slabs.\n- **DGU / IGU:** double or insulated glazing unit.\n- **Toughened (tempered) glass:** heat-treated, breaks into small pieces.\n- **Laminated glass:** layers bonded with PVB or SGP, and stays in place when broken.\n- **Heat-strengthened glass:** between annealed and toughened.\n- **Low-E:** a low-emissivity coating.\n- **U-value (W/m²K):** heat transfer; lower is better.\n- **SHGC / SC:** solar heat gain coefficient / shading coefficient; lower means less solar heat. SC ≈ SHGC / 0.87.\n- **VLT:** visible light transmittance.\n- **Thermal break:** an insulating strip in an aluminium profile.\n- **Powder coating / anodising:** aluminium finishes.\n- **Rolling shutter (garage-doors):** slat curtain on a barrel; it can be manual or motorised. Fire-rated shutters need the fire certificates in section 2.\n- **MRL lift:** machine-room-less. **Rated load (kg / persons), speed (m/s), stops:** the core lift spec.\n- **Cast vs extruded acrylic (PMMA):** cast is harder and better for fabricated signs.\n\n## 8. What a buyer should check in a quote\n\n1. **Facade or cladding:** the quote names the exact panel or system and attaches the Civil Defence approval or ESL certificate of conformity for that product (Dubai) or the ADCD approval (Abu Dhabi), plus the system test reference. A brochure claim is not enough.\n2. **Facade contractor:** the quote states the contractor's Civil Defence approval or licence, if the project needs one (reported requirement; confirm with the consultant).\n3. **Glazing:** the quote states glass make-up (for example 6 mm toughened Low-E + 12 mm air + 6 mm clear), U-value, SHGC or SC, VLT, and which panes are toughened or laminated. It matches the consultant's Al Sa'fat or energy compliance sheet, and DM glass certification is included where required.\n4. **Aluminium:** the quote names the profile system, alloy and temper, thickness, finish and its warranty, thermal break (yes or no), hardware brand, and sealant and gasket types.\n5. **Regulated products:** the quote has an ECAS or EQM certificate number, checkable on the MoIAT register, and it is still valid, since certificates last one year.\n6. **Lifts:**\n   - rated load, speed, stops and drive type\n   - whether the supplier is DM-registered (Dubai)\n   - the first examination and certificate: who pays, and whether by an accredited body\n   - the maintenance contract: term, response time and price after the warranty. In Abu Dhabi, no contract means a fine.\n7. **Signage:** the quote says who obtains the permit and pays its fee, whether the design matches the permitted size, location and content, and covers material (cast or extruded acrylic, ACP, LED), illumination, structure or fixing, and removal at the end of the permit.\n8. **All trades:** the quote states what is included (design, shop drawings, authority submissions, testing, installation, scaffolding), the warranty period and terms, and the validity of the offer.\n\n## Unverified items (summary)\n\n- Chapter and section location of the facade rules in the 2018 code.\n- Wording of the \"ten-point\" approach.\n- Whether ESL fully replaced the Dubai Central Laboratory route, and the 2023 deadline.\n- All numeric Dubai glazing limits (U-value, SC, SHGC, VLT).\n- Al Sa'fat edition date.\n- Content of DM-DCLD-RD-DP32-5103.\n- Safety-glass mandatory locations.\n- Which glass and aluminium products are MoIAT-regulated.\n- Dubai lift inspection interval, and the registered-company and EIAC details.\n- Everything about Abu Dhabi lift inspection, and the coverage of ADS 22/2017.\n- The Mada Media platform and the per-sign permit wording.\n- Sign size limits.\n- Abu Dhabi signage and envelope rules (not researched).\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/fire_safety_and_security.md>>>\n# UAE fire & life safety and security systems: regulatory digest\n\n- **Scope:** trade categories `safety` (fire & life safety systems: fire alarms, sprinklers, suppression, extinguishers, emergency lighting, fire doors) and `security` (CCTV, access control, intrusion alarms).\n- **Compiled:** 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest.\n- **Rule for agents:** cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.\n- **Conventions:** every source was accessed on 2026-10-12. Anything marked \"(unverified)\" comes from secondary or commercial sources only. Do not state it to a customer as fact. Clause numbers are given only where the source names them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fire & life safety\n\n### 1.1 Governing code and authorities\n\n| Item | What is verified | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| National code | The **UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice**. DCD publishes the **September 2018** edition as a PDF; the DCD page is dated 24 Mar 2025. | https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/preventive-safety/uae-fire-and-life-safety-code-of-practice.jsp |\n| Dubai authority | **Dubai Civil Defence (DCD)**. Its online services include \"Company Approval\", \"Engineering Drawings\" approval and \"Hassantuk\". | https://www.dcd.gov.ae/ ; https://smartservices.dcd.gov.ae/en |\n| Abu Dhabi authority | **Abu Dhabi Civil Defence Authority (ADCDA)**. It requires installation and maintenance by companies that Civil Defence licenses, done by registered technicians and engineers who have passed the required tests. | https://www.adpolice.gov.ae/en/Media-Center/News/2021/07/01/-Civil-Defense-Abu-Dhabi--urges-building-owners-to-adopt-maintenance-contracts-for-prevention-and-sa |\n| Federal basis | Cabinet Resolution No. 24 of 2012 on organising civil defence services. ADCDA cites **clause 91** of it for the AED 50,000 fine below. Reported by press, not read in the resolution itself. | https://gulfnews.com/uae/emergencies/abu-dhabi-civil-defence-dh50000-fine-for-unlicensed-fire-safety-activities-1.103754127 |\n| Other emirates | Each emirate's Civil Defence applies the UAE Code. Sharjah, Ajman, RAK, UAQ and Fujairah procedures were not researched. (unverified) | n/a |\n\n### 1.2 Approvals installers and products must hold\n\n- **Contractor approval (Dubai).** Fire-system companies need DCD \"Company Approval\". Source: https://smartservices.dcd.gov.ae/en\n  - A trade licence alone does not let a company design, install, test or maintain fire systems. (unverified, business-setup blog: https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/fire-safety-company-setup-dubai)\n  - Approval also depends on a qualified fire-protection engineer who has passed DCD exams. (unverified, same blog)\n- **Contractor approval (Abu Dhabi).**\n  - Installation and maintenance must be done by Civil Defence-licensed companies, with registered and tested technicians. Source: ADPolice link in 1.1.\n  - Manufacturing, trading, installing or maintaining fire safety systems without Civil Defence approval carries an **AED 50,000 fine**, as reported by press. Source: Gulf News link in 1.1.\n- **Maintenance contract (Abu Dhabi).**\n  - Owners must keep a maintenance contract with an approved contractor. Source: ADPolice link in 1.1.\n  - The penalty for having no valid contract is reported as **AED 10,000**. (unverified, secondary: https://www.lexis.ae/2023/07/26/abu-dhabi-fire-safety-fine-reminder/)\n- **Firestopping (Abu Dhabi, 2025 circular).** Specialty firestop contractors need **FM 4991 Approved** or **UL Qualified Firestop Contractor** certification. Suppliers that issue engineering judgments need the UL Technical Evaluation Developer Program. Source: copy of the ADCDA circular hosted by FCIA: https://www.fcia.org/Portals/0/2025%20ADCDA%20Circular%20Firestop%20and%20Through%20Penetration%20Systems%20%281%29.pdf\n- **Product approval.** Fire products (detection, suppression, emergency lighting and others) are certified under the UAE Code before they may be used.\n  - Certification goes through Civil Defence-recognised certification bodies or labs, such as the Emirates Safety Laboratory. (unverified, BSI exporter guide and press: https://www.bsigroup.com/globalassets/localfiles/en-in/resources/exporters_uae_civil_defence_web.pdf ; https://gulfnews.com/uae/dubai-announces-new-fire-safety-standards-1.95253431)\n  - Buyer action: ask the supplier for the product's Civil Defence certificate or listing for the emirate where the work is done.\n- **Combustible and insulation materials (Dubai).** A Dubai Municipality-hosted circular says materials such as rock wool, polystyrene and bitumen may be used only with DCD approval and per the latest UAE Code. Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/5-1-1-Concerning-Fire-Prevention.pdf\n- **MoIAT ECAS/EQM.** This is the federal conformity scheme for regulated products: a certificate valid for 1 year, specific to the product and its factory.\n  - No source confirmed that extinguishers or detectors fall under ECAS. Treat Civil Defence approval as the controlling requirement for fire products. (unverified)\n  - Sources: https://www.tuvsud.com/en-ae/services/product-certification/moiat-ecas-product-certification-services-in-uae ; https://www.sgs.com/en-ae/news/2024/06/pca-2024-q2-navigating-uae-product-conformity-understanding-ecas-and-eqm-certification\n- **Hassantuk.** A DCD-listed service for connected fire alarm monitoring. Which premises it applies to was not verified. Source: https://smartservices.dcd.gov.ae/en (scope unverified)\n\n### 1.3 Thresholds and limits\n\n- Sprinkler, detection and emergency-lighting thresholds (building height, area, occupancy), and fire-rating durations for doors and walls, are set in the 2018 Code text. They were **not extracted for this digest**.\n- Do not quote figures such as \"60 min door\" or \"sprinklers above X m\" as regulation. Refer the buyer to the consultant's DCD/ADCDA-approved drawings. (unverified)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Security systems (CCTV, access control, intrusion)\n\n### 2.1 Governing law and authority\n\n| Item | What is verified | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dubai regulator | **SIRA**, the Security Industry Regulatory Agency. It regulates the private security sector in Dubai, including security systems, and keeps a directory of licensed companies. | https://www.sira.gov.ae/ |\n| Dubai law | **Law No. (12) of 2016** Regulating the Security Industry in the Emirate of Dubai. It defines \"Preventive Systems\" (electronic systems and security equipment). | https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2016/Law%20No.%20(12)%20of%202016.html |\n| Implementing bylaw | Board of Directors **Resolution No. (1) of 2018**. It specifies which establishments must have systems. | https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2018/Resolution%20No.%20(1)%20of%202018.html |\n| Technical standard | **Administrative Resolution No. (13) of 2025**, the **Preventive Systems Manual**. It was issued 14 Apr 2025 and took effect on Official Gazette publication (Article 4). It applies to the establishments in Resolution 1/2018 (Article 2) and sets minimum specs for CCTV, access control, alarms, locks, glass and vehicle barriers, plus sector rules for hotels, malls, banks, data centres and others. | https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2025/Administrative%20Resolution%20No.%20(13)%20of%202025%20the%20Preventive%20System%20Manual.html |\n| Abu Dhabi and other emirates | Security-systems licensing outside Dubai (Abu Dhabi Police / Monitoring & Control Centre and others) was not researched. (unverified) | n/a |\n\n### 2.2 Approvals and obligations (Dubai, Preventive Systems Manual, same source as above)\n\n- **Maintenance contract.** The establishment must hold an **annual maintenance contract with a SIRA-registered company**, including preventive maintenance. It must tell SIRA if the contractor does not perform.\n- **Technician licensing.** The whole technical maintenance crew must be **licensed by SIRA**, and providers must keep their crew details up to date with SIRA.\n- **Change notification.** Any change to the system, its layout or the premises, however minor, must be notified to SIRA and recorded.\n- **Deviations.** Anything below the Manual's minimum specs needs official SIRA approval.\n- **Recorders.** Recording devices must be kept in secure, monitored locations with an auditable access log.\n- **Warning signs.** SIRA-approved CCTV warning signs are required at main entrances and inside lifts.\n- **Wireless components.** Wireless sensors and alarm components generally need prior SIRA approval.\n- **Company licence.** Security-system installers and maintainers need a SIRA licence; the SIRA site has a company directory.\n  - Reported fees of AED 220–520 per activity are unverified (consultancy blog: https://www.rizmona.com/blog/sira-license-dubai-uae/).\n  - A reported one-year accreditation period is also unverified.\n\n### 2.3 Key numbers (Dubai, Preventive Systems Manual)\n\n- CCTV recordings must be kept for **at least 31 days**, at high quality and **at least 10 frames per second**.\n- Hotel door-lock systems must keep **at least six months** of movement records.\n- The \"30–90 days storage\" figure seen on blogs is **not** the rule. Use 31 days minimum, plus any sector-specific rule in the Manual.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Glossary (brief)\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| DCD / ADCDA | Dubai Civil Defence / Abu Dhabi Civil Defence Authority: the fire-safety approval authorities |\n| UAE FLS Code | UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice (DCD publishes the Sept 2018 edition) |\n| FACP | Fire alarm control panel |\n| Addressable vs conventional | Addressable: each detector reports its own address. Conventional: the panel reports by zone only |\n| Clean agent / FM-200 / Novec / CO2 | Gaseous suppression systems, typically for server and electrical rooms |\n| Wet / dry / pre-action / deluge | Sprinkler system types |\n| FRL / fire rating (e.g. 60/90/120 min) | How long a door, wall or damper resists fire, per its test certificate |\n| Firestopping | Sealing of penetrations through fire-rated walls and floors |\n| AMC | Annual maintenance contract |\n| SIRA | Security Industry Regulatory Agency (Dubai) |\n| Preventive Systems | Legal term (Dubai Law 12/2016) for security systems and equipment |\n| NVR/DVR, VMS | Network/digital video recorder; video management software |\n| ACS / ANPR | Access control system; automatic number-plate recognition |\n| ECAS / EQM | MoIAT federal product conformity certificate / quality mark |\n\n---\n\n## 4. What a buyer should check in a quote\n\n1. **Fire works.** The contractor's current Civil Defence approval for the emirate (DCD Company Approval, or ADCDA licence) and the approval category that matches the scope (install, maintain, firestop).\n2. **Security works in Dubai.** The company appears in SIRA's licensed directory, and its technicians hold SIRA licences.\n3. **Products.** Make, model and Civil Defence certificate or listing for every fire item (detectors, panels, sprinkler heads, extinguishers, fire doors, emergency lights). For electrical items, check ECAS/EQM where it applies.\n4. **Fire doors and firestopping.** The tested fire rating in minutes, the certificate, and installation per the tested system. In Abu Dhabi, FM 4991 or UL QFC for firestop contractors.\n5. **Drawings and approvals.** Who submits the drawings to DCD/ADCDA, who books inspections, and who obtains the completion or approval certificate. All of this should be priced in the quote.\n6. **CCTV specs (Dubai).** Recording retention of at least 31 days, at least 10 fps, storage sized to match, secure recorder location, SIRA signage, and whether SIRA approval or notification is included.\n7. **AMC.** Price and term of the annual maintenance contract, preventive-maintenance visit frequency, and response time. An AMC is mandatory for Dubai security systems and Abu Dhabi fire systems.\n8. **Exclusions.** Civil works, power, network cabling, authority fees and re-inspection fees.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sources (all accessed 2026-10-12)\n\n**Official**\n- https://www.dcd.gov.ae/\n- https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/preventive-safety/uae-fire-and-life-safety-code-of-practice.jsp\n- https://smartservices.dcd.gov.ae/en\n- https://www.adpolice.gov.ae/en/Media-Center/News/2021/07/01/-Civil-Defense-Abu-Dhabi--urges-building-owners-to-adopt-maintenance-contracts-for-prevention-and-sa\n- https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/5-1-1-Concerning-Fire-Prevention.pdf\n- https://www.sira.gov.ae/\n- https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2016/Law%20No.%20(12)%20of%202016.html\n- https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2018/Resolution%20No.%20(1)%20of%202018.html\n- https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2025/Administrative%20Resolution%20No.%20(13)%20of%202025%20the%20Preventive%20System%20Manual.html\n\n**Authoritative secondary** (ADCDA circular copy, notified bodies, press)\n- https://www.fcia.org/Portals/0/2025%20ADCDA%20Circular%20Firestop%20and%20Through%20Penetration%20Systems%20%281%29.pdf\n- https://gulfnews.com/uae/emergencies/abu-dhabi-civil-defence-dh50000-fine-for-unlicensed-fire-safety-activities-1.103754127\n- https://gulfnews.com/uae/dubai-announces-new-fire-safety-standards-1.95253431\n- https://www.bsigroup.com/globalassets/localfiles/en-in/resources/exporters_uae_civil_defence_web.pdf\n- https://www.tuvsud.com/en-ae/services/product-certification/moiat-ecas-product-certification-services-in-uae\n- https://www.sgs.com/en-ae/news/2024/06/pca-2024-q2-navigating-uae-product-conformity-understanding-ecas-and-eqm-certification\n\n**Commercial** (unverified items only)\n- https://www.lexis.ae/2023/07/26/abu-dhabi-fire-safety-fine-reminder/\n- https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/fire-safety-company-setup-dubai\n- https://www.rizmona.com/blog/sira-license-dubai-uae/\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/hvac_plumbing_and_mep.md>>>\n# UAE regulations digest: HVAC, plumbing and MEP\n\n**Scope (category codes):** hvac, plumbing, mep, combined-mep, insulation, water-tank-cleaning, swimming-pools\n**Compiled:** 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest.\n**Agents:** cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.\n\nAll sources were accessed on 2026-10-12. Anything marked \"(unverified)\" could not be confirmed from an official or primary source. Do not present it to a buyer as fact. No clause numbers are given unless the source states them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Authorities and codes by emirate\n\n### Dubai\n- **Dubai Building Code (DBC).** Created by Decree No. (45) of 2021, which defines it as minimum design requirements for public health, safety and sustainability. Dubai Municipality (DM) publishes it.\n  Source: https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2021/Decree%20No.%20(45)%20of%202021%20Concerning%20the%20Dubai%20Building%20Code.html\n- **DEWA.** A DEWA circular (17 Jan 2022) says DBC compliance is mandatory and is checked when drawings are submitted for DEWA design approval on every project in Dubai.\n  Source: https://dewa.gov.ae/-/media/Files/Consultants-and-Contractors/Circulars/Dubai-Building-Code-Circular-EN.ashx\n- **Trakhees (PCFC zones).** The DBC has been mandatory for permit applications in Trakhees jurisdiction since 30 Dec 2021.\n  Source: https://pcfc.ae/en/Rules%20and%20Regulations/Trakhees/Civil%20Engineering%20Department/Permits%20(Facilities)/Circulars/CEDGR-135%20Dubai%20Building%20Code%20Circular%202021.pdf\n- **The DBC part letters that cover HVAC and plumbing (unverified).** Check the DBC table of contents on dm.gov.ae.\n- **Al Sa'fat (Dubai Green Building System).** It replaced the Dubai Green Building Regulations from 19 Oct 2020. Every new building must meet the mandatory requirements for **Silver Sa'fa**. **Golden** and **Platinum Sa'fa** are optional. The current edition is the 2nd Edition (Jan 2023), aligned with the DBC. Administrative Resolution No. (154) of 2020 approved the Al Sa'fat bylaw. The government-stated energy savings are 19% for Silver, 32% for Gold and 35% for Platinum. Al Sa'fat directly drives HVAC efficiency, insulation and water-fixture specifications.\n  Sources: https://www.dm.gov.ae/municipality-business/al-safat-dubai-green-building-system/ ; https://mediaoffice.ae/en/news/2023/December/07-12/Green-Buildings-in-Dubai\n  - Certificate validity periods (unverified; from a property blog only).\n  - \"Silver Sa'fa is required to get a building permit\" (unverified as worded; an official source confirms only that Silver Sa'fa is mandatory for new buildings).\n\n### Abu Dhabi\n- **Estidama Pearl Rating System (DMT).** All new projects must reach at least **1 Pearl** to get planning and permitting approval. Government-funded buildings, as defined in Information Bulletin #1, need at least **2 Pearls**. 1 Pearl means every mandatory credit is met. 2 to 5 Pearls also need credit points.\n  Source: https://pages.dmt.gov.ae/en/Urban-Planning/The-Pearl-Rating-System-for-Estidama\n  - \"Villas must reach 2 Pearls\" (unverified; found in one secondary source only).\n\n### Federal (all emirates)\n- **AC energy-efficiency labelling (MoIAT, formerly ESMA).** Cabinet Resolution No. (157) of 2025 sets the technical regulation for AC energy efficiency. It lists **UAE.S 5010-1:2025** for low-capacity (household) units and **UAE.S 5010-5:2025** for large-capacity units. Labels use a **1 to 5 star** scale, where 5 is the most efficient, and products are certified under the **ECAS** scheme or the voluntary **EQM** scheme.\n  Sources: https://uaelegislation.gov.ae/en/legislations/3862/download ; https://www.tuv.com/market-access-services/en/certification-filter/uae-moiat-energy-efficiency-standardization-labeling-(eesl).html\n  - The 2025 star bands and minimum efficiency (MEPS) values are unverified. Older 2014 bands (split units: 5 stars from EER 9.01) are superseded. Do not quote them as current.\n    Source for the 2014 bands: https://www.iea.org/policies/6901-uaes-5010-12014-household-air-conditioners\n- **Refrigerants.** Decree No. 138 of 2023 regulates **HFCs**, with a progressive phase-down that reportedly aims at a full ban by 2040 (law-firm summary).\n  Source: https://hadefpartners.com/news-insights/insights/road-to-cop28-another-important-step-towards-phasing-out-the-use-of-hydrofluorocarbons-hfcs-in-the-uae/\n  - A UAE ban on importing R-22 (HCFC) equipment, its date, and any technician F-gas licensing scheme are all unverified. The UAE is a Montreal Protocol party that is phasing out HCFCs.\n\n### Other emirates (Sharjah, Ajman, RAK, UAQ, Fujairah)\n- Each municipality issues its own building permits. Their HVAC and plumbing code adoption, and any green-building rules, are unverified in this digest. Tell the buyer to confirm with their municipality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Approvals and certificates contractors and products should hold\n\n| Item | What to ask for | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Any contractor | A valid trade licence covering the activity (from DET in Dubai, or the emirate's economic department) | General UAE practice |\n| Dubai MEP or building works | DM-registered contractor classification for permit work | (unverified detail) |\n| Dubai connections to DEWA | Drawings approved by DEWA, DBC compliance checked at submission | Verified (DEWA circular) |\n| AC units (household and large) | MoIAT/ECAS conformity and an energy label with stars | Verified (Res. 157/2025) |\n| Water tank cleaning, Dubai | Company approved by Dubai Municipality and on DM's approved list of cleaning and disinfection companies | Reported in press; list location per a 2022 guide (see §3) |\n| Pool lifeguards, Dubai | Certificate from a training company accredited by EIAC | Reported 2017 (see §3) |\n| Fire-related MEP (sprinklers, pumps) | Civil Defence approval | (unverified; out of scope here) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Category notes\n\n### water-tank-cleaning\n- In Dubai, tank cleaning must be done by **companies approved by Dubai Municipality**. DM vets them on staff training, equipment and cleaning procedure. A buyer can ask for proof of approval, and DM publishes a list of approved cleaning and disinfection companies.\n  Sources: https://gulfnews.com/uae/environment/new-rules-to-ensure-water-tanks-are-kept-clean-1.1004260 ; https://gulfnews.com/living-in-uae/housing/dubai-how-do-i-know-that-my-homes-water-tank-is-clean-1.1655883123621\n- Frequency: tanks should be cleaned and disinfected **at least twice a year**, per the Dubai Food Code 2020 as reported in the press (unverified against the code text).\n- A lab test of the water after cleaning, and before-and-after photos, are good practice (unverified as a legal requirement).\n\n### swimming-pools\n- **Dubai, public pools.** Local Order No. 11 of 2003 makes the owner or manager responsible for health and safety conditions, including safety equipment and a qualified lifeguard. Local Order No. 2 of 2012 amended this, and the full-time lifeguard duty does not apply to pools in residential buildings or residential compounds.\n  Source: https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2012/Local%20Order%20%282%29%20of%202012_Final_amended.html\n- From 2017, lifeguard certificates had to come from training providers accredited by EIAC (press report).\n  Source: https://gulfnews.com/business/tourism/certified-lifeguards-must-for-pools-in-dubai-by-july-1.1968489\n- Rules on children's use of hotel pools changed over time and the current rule is unverified. Rules on pool water-quality parameters are also unverified.\n\n### hvac\n- Equipment must carry the MoIAT energy label. Building design must meet the DBC and Al Sa'fat (Dubai) or Estidama (Abu Dhabi).\n- Refrigerant type matters. HFC use is being phased down (see §1).\n\n### plumbing, mep, combined-mep\n- In Dubai, design must comply with the DBC, and DEWA approval is needed for water and electricity connections. Al Sa'fat sets water-efficiency expectations for fixtures. Specific flow-rate limits are unverified here.\n\n### insulation (thermal)\n- Thermal-envelope and duct or pipe insulation requirements sit in Al Sa'fat and the DBC (Dubai) and in Estidama (Abu Dhabi). Specific U-values or R-values are unverified here. Ask the consultant for the project's compliance sheet.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Glossary (brief)\n- **EER / SEER / CSPF**: cooling efficiency ratios. Higher is better. UAE labels convert these into stars.\n- **TR (ton of refrigeration)**: about 3.517 kW of cooling. Used in UAE HVAC quotes.\n- **BTU/h**: the unit for split-unit capacity. 12,000 BTU/h is 1 TR.\n- **VRF/VRV**: a variable refrigerant flow multi-split system.\n- **FCU / AHU / FAHU**: fan coil unit / air handling unit / fresh-air handling unit.\n- **Chiller / district cooling**: central chilled-water plant. Many Dubai and Abu Dhabi towers buy chilled water from a district cooling provider.\n- **MEP**: mechanical, electrical, plumbing.\n- **PPR / uPVC / CPVC / HDPE**: common pipe materials.\n- **GRP tank**: a glass-reinforced plastic sectional water tank, common on UAE rooftops.\n- **Silver/Gold/Platinum Sa'fa**: Dubai green tiers. **Pearl 1 to 5**: Abu Dhabi Estidama tiers.\n- **ECAS / EQM**: the UAE conformity schemes (mandatory and voluntary).\n\n---\n\n## 5. What a buyer should check in a quote\n1. Trade licence activity matches the work, and the licence is valid.\n2. For AC supply: brand, model, capacity (TR or BTU/h), **star rating and MoIAT/ECAS label**, refrigerant type, and warranty (compressor warranty stated separately).\n3. Whether design or shop drawings and authority submissions (DM, DEWA, Trakhees, DMT, Civil Defence) are included, or are the client's responsibility.\n4. Whether the quote states the green-building tier the work must meet: Silver Sa'fa as the Dubai minimum, or 1 Pearl as the Abu Dhabi minimum (2 Pearls for government buildings).\n5. A materials schedule (pipe type, insulation thickness and type, duct class), along with testing and commissioning, and handover documents.\n6. Water tank cleaning: proof of DM approval (Dubai), the scope (draining, disinfection, lab test, photos), and the visit frequency.\n7. Pools: the equipment list, the water-treatment system, and who supplies the lifeguard or safety equipment. For public pools this is the owner's duty under the Dubai Local Orders.\n8. Exclusions, payment terms, programme dates, and the defects-liability period.\n\n---\n\n## Unverified items (summary)\n- The DBC part letters for HVAC and plumbing, and its specific numeric limits.\n- Validity periods for Al Sa'fat certificates. Whether Silver Sa'fa is worded as a permit precondition.\n- The Estidama 2-Pearl rule for villas.\n- The star bands and MEPS values in UAE.S 5010-1/-5:2025.\n- An R-22 import ban and its date. A technician refrigerant licensing scheme.\n- The water tank cleaning frequency as written in the Food Code text. Whether a lab test is legally required.\n- The current hotel pool child rules. Pool water-quality parameters.\n- DM contractor classification details. Civil Defence MEP approvals.\n- Rules in the northern emirates.\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/interiors_and_fitout.md>>>\n# UAE regulations and materials primer: interiors and fit-out\n\n**Scope (category codes):** fitout, commercial-retail-fitout, interior-design, gypsum, flooring, tiling, epoxy-flooring, marble-granite, carpets, painting, wallpaper, blinds-curtains, carpentry-joinery, custom-furniture, kitchens, upholstery\n\ncompiled 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest\n\n**Agents: cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.**\n\nAll sources accessed 2026-10-12. Items marked \"(unverified)\" came only from secondary or commercial sources, or are not confirmed in an official text. No clause numbers are given unless the source states them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Which authority approves a fit-out\n\nJurisdiction depends on **where the unit is**, not on the type of business. Before quoting, a supplier should confirm which authority applies.\n\n| Area | Approving body | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Most of mainland Dubai | Dubai Municipality (DM) fit-out / building-modification permit | Official DM permit portal not fetched here. The scope description (\"partitions, ceilings, flooring, MEP, finishes\") comes from secondary guides **(unverified)**. Source: https://www.dubizzle.com/blog/property/dubai-fit-out-approvals/ |\n| DDA zones (e.g. Dubai Internet City, Media City, Design District, other TECOM clusters) | Dubai Development Authority (DDA) fit-out permit | Official page, see §2. https://dda.gov.ae/en/planning-development/construction/permits-nocs/fit-out-permit |\n| Trakhees areas (Ports, Customs & Free Zone Corporation: e.g. JAFZA, and Nakheel communities such as Palm Jumeirah) | Trakhees (PCFC) Civil Engineering modification NOC/permit | The Trakhees licensing rules state that a Civil Engineering Department modification NOC/permit is mandatory before any modification or fit-out work. https://www.pcfc.ae/en/Rules%20and%20Regulations/Trakhees/Licensing%20Department/Licensing/Rules%20&%20Regulations/PCFC-TRK-LD-LS-REG02,%20Operational%20Requirements%20for%20Trakhees.pdf . The full list of Trakhees communities is **(unverified)**. |\n| Nakheel master communities | Nakheel master-developer NOC / stamped drawings, which then go to Trakhees | No standalone \"Nakheel fit-out permit\" was found. Nakheel's role appears to be the NOC and drawing stamp that feed the Trakhees permit **(unverified)**. |\n| Fire and life safety (all of Dubai) | Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) | DCD approval and completion are separate from the fit-out permit (see §2 and §3). |\n| Other emirates | Each emirate's municipality, civil defence and free-zone authority | Not researched here. Do not apply Dubai rules to Abu Dhabi, Sharjah or the other emirates. |\n\n**Typical sequence (general):** landlord or building-management NOC, then authority permit, then work starts, then inspections and a completion certificate. Work should not start before the permit is issued. Source for the sequence: secondary guides **(unverified as an official rule outside DDA and Trakhees)**.\n\n**Contractor licensing:** Secondary sources mention a Dubai Contractor Register and contractor grading under \"Dubai Law No. 7 of 2025\" from January 2026. **(unverified: not confirmed against an official text.)** Source: https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/interior-fit-out-company-dubai . Interior design consultancy and fit-out contracting are usually separate licence activities **(unverified)**.\n\n## 2. DDA fit-out permit (official page)\n\nSource: https://dda.gov.ae/en/planning-development/construction/permits-nocs/fit-out-permit (accessed 2026-10-12)\n\n- **Documents:** permit request form (customer-service-centre route only); contractor appointment letter issued by the tenant; EJARI copy; NOC from the building owner; NOCs from relevant authorities (per DDA's NOC matrix); approved stamped drawings and proposed drawings per DDA \"Circular No. 400\".\n- **Conditions:**\n  - A Dubai Civil Defence completion certificate is required before the fit-out completion certificate is issued.\n  - Coring under 200 mm must not affect structural elements such as tendons and must be at least 1 m from a column.\n  - Adding structural elements, changing the building's use, or adding gross floor area requires a building modification request, not a fit-out permit. If DDA reclassifies a submission this way, the fit-out fees are not refunded.\n  - If the building has no completion certificate yet, NOCs from the main contractor, main consultant and plot owner are also required.\n- **Validity:** 6 months.\n- A DDA circular (https://dda.gov.ae/-/media/116360B0DDBF4E1E89473C90A530CEAD.pdf) is reported to exempt minor works such as internal painting from the fit-out permit. **(unverified: the circular's text was not read.)**\n\n## 3. Fire safety: materials, curtains and upholstery\n\n- **Dubai Civil Defence circular \"5-1-1 Concerning Fire Prevention\" (2024 upload on dm.gov.ae):** it prohibits flammable materials, or materials with flammable components, unless the General Directorate of Civil Defence in Dubai has approved them under the latest **UAE Fire and Life Safety Code**. The examples it gives are insulation (rock wool, polystyrene) and fixing materials. The circular is addressed to consultants, contractors, suppliers and developers. https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/5-1-1-Concerning-Fire-Prevention.pdf\n- **Curtains, drapes, blinds, upholstery and carpets:** no Dubai text was found that names these items. They fall under the general rule above. The specific test classes the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code requires for interior textiles and finishes were **not verified** (the code text was not read). Practical guidance for agents:\n  - For commercial, hospitality and public spaces, buyers usually ask for **flame-retardant (FR) certified fabric** with a test report and DCD-acceptable certification. Agents should ask the supplier to state the standard tested to (e.g. NFPA 701 or BS 5867 for curtains; BS 5852 for upholstery) **(general industry knowledge; acceptance by DCD unverified)**.\n  - Some DCD-regulated products must be certified via an approved laboratory. A news report names an \"Emirates Safety Laboratory\" **(unverified scope)**. https://gulfnews.com/uae/dubai-announces-new-fire-safety-standards-1.95253431\n  - Where the UAE code is silent, DCD has reportedly fallen back on NFPA standards (news source, older) **(unverified)**. https://www.gulf-times.com/story/529977/dubai-toughens-fire-rules-after-tower-blazes\n- **Fire-rated items in fit-out:** fire-rated doors, fire-rated gypsum partitions and ceiling systems, and fire-stopping normally need DCD-approved products, and the DCD approval should be listed in the quote. **(general industry practice; specific listing rules unverified)**\n\n## 4. Paint and coatings: VOC limits (Dubai Al Sa'fat)\n\nDubai Municipality's green building system **Al Sa'fat** (2017 edition referenced) sets limits for low-emitting paints, coatings, adhesives and sealants. **Dubai Central Laboratory (DCL)** issues product conformity certificates against it. The figures below are **taken from DCL certificates** that quote the Al Sa'fat limits, not from the regulation text. Check the current Al Sa'fat edition before relying on them.\n\n| Product subcategory (as stated on certificates) | Max VOC |\n|---|---|\n| Interior matt walls/ceilings (gloss < 25 @ 60°) | 30 g/L |\n| Exterior walls, mineral substrate | 40 g/L |\n| Multi-coloured water-based coatings | 100 g/L |\n| One-pack water-based performance coatings | 140 g/L |\n| Two-pack reactive / floor coatings (solvent-based) | 500 g/L |\n\nThe same certificates also show these limits: heavy metals (Cd, Cr VI < 500 mg/kg; As, Hg, Pb < 100 mg/kg) and formaldehyde (< 10 mg/kg).\nSources: https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/CL17020457.pdf ; https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/CL18020619.pdf ; https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/CL18020628.pdf\n\n**Relevance to agents:** epoxy-flooring and two-pack floor coatings fall in the 500 g/L band. Buyers on green-rated or government projects may ask for a **DCL certificate** for paint, adhesives (tiling, flooring, wallpaper) and sealants.\n\n## 5. Federal product conformity (MoIAT / ECAS)\n\n- The **Ministry of Industry and Advanced Technology (MoIAT)** runs **ECAS** (Emirates Conformity Assessment Scheme, mandatory for regulated products, certificate valid 1 year) and **EQM** (Emirates Quality Mark, certificate valid 3 years). Regulated products need one of these to be sold in the UAE. https://www.sgs.com/en-ae/news/2024/06/pca-2024-q2-navigating-uae-product-conformity-understanding-ecas-and-eqm-certification\n- **Paints and varnishes** are certified under ECAS against the technical requirements of **Cabinet Resolution No. 6 of 2016**, as described by a certification body. Whether this resolution is current or has been amended is **(unverified)**. https://cdn3-verigates.bureauveritas.com/sites/verigates/files/2023-02/AE%20-%20MoIAT%20Service%20Cards%2009022023.pdf\n- New ECAS logos and conformity marks were set by **Ministerial Resolution No. 137 of 2024**, with a 6-month transition for new imports and 1 year for existing stock. https://www.sgs.com/en-iq/news/2024/11/moiat-announces-new-logo-and-conformity-marks-under-ministerial-resolution-no-137-of-2024\n- **Wood-based panels (MDF, particleboard, plywood):** no UAE regulation setting a formaldehyde emission class was found. A certification body's MoIAT service list includes a \"Wood Materials\" category, but its requirements were not found. **(unverified: do not state a UAE-mandated E-class.)**\n\n## 6. Materials primer (general industry knowledge)\n\nThese are not UAE regulations. Use them to explain options to buyers.\n\n**Wood panels (carpentry-joinery, kitchens, custom-furniture)**\n- **MDF (medium-density fibreboard):** made from fine wood fibres and resin. Smooth and uniform, it machines and paints well (good for lacquered doors and CNC routing). It is heavy, holds screws poorly at the edges, and swells if water gets in.\n- **MR MDF (moisture-resistant, often green-cored):** has better swell resistance and is used for kitchens, vanities and humid areas. It is **not waterproof**: edges must be sealed.\n- **Particleboard / chipboard:** made from wood chips and resin. It is the cheapest option and is usually supplied melamine-faced (MFC) for carcasses. It has the weakest screw holding and moisture tolerance.\n- **Plywood:** cross-laminated veneers. It has the best strength-to-weight ratio and screw holding. Marine or WBP grades use water-boil-proof glue for wet areas. Its surface is less smooth than MDF for paint.\n- **Formaldehyde emission classes:** **E1** (EN 13986 / EN 717-1 chamber test, ≤ 0.1 ppm, about 0.124 mg/m³) is the European baseline. **E0** and **\"super E0\"** are lower industry or market labels (commonly cited for Asian and Chinese grades). **CARB Phase 2 / TSCA Title VI** is the US limit regime. Ask for the test certificate, not just the label.\n  Sources: https://www.epa.gov/formaldehyde/formaldehyde-emission-standards-composite-wood-products ; https://ww2.arb.ca.gov/our-work/programs/composite-wood-products-program\n\n**Tiles and stone (tiling, flooring, marble-granite)**\n- **Porcelain vs ceramic:** porcelain is fired denser, with water absorption **≤ 0.5%** (ISO 13006 group BIa). It is harder and frost-resistant, suits floors, wet areas and outdoors, and is harder to cut. Ceramic is more porous (higher absorption) and is usually used on walls and for light-traffic floors. Full-body porcelain shows less wear on chips than glazed porcelain. Source: https://www.iso.org/standard/60268.html\n- **Slip resistance:** buyers may ask for an R-rating (DIN 51130, R9 to R13) or a pendulum (PTV) value for wet areas and exteriors.\n- **Granite:** a natural igneous stone. It is very hard and resistant to heat and scratching, and should be sealed periodically.\n- **Marble:** a softer calcareous stone. It **etches with acids** (lemon, vinegar, some cleaners) and stains, needs sealing, and can be honed or polished.\n- **Quartz (engineered stone):** about 90%+ crushed quartz bound with resin. It is non-porous with no sealing needed and gives consistent patterns. It is **less heat-tolerant** (resin can scorch, so use trivets) and is not UV-stable outdoors. Fabrication involves respirable crystalline silica, so cutting needs dust control and wet methods. Source: https://www.osha.gov/silica-crystalline\n- **Sintered stone / porcelain slabs:** heat- and UV-resistant and very hard. Edges can chip.\n\n**Other trades**\n- **Gypsum board:** standard; moisture-resistant (MR, usually green) for bathrooms and kitchens; fire-rated (FR, usually pink or red, type X). The fire-resistance rating comes from the **tested system** (studs, layers, insulation), not from the board alone. **(general industry knowledge)**\n- **Epoxy flooring:** comes as a self-levelling, roller-coat or mortar screed system. It needs a dry, primed substrate, so the concrete moisture test should be stated in the quote. PU and PU-cement systems handle heat and thermal shock better (kitchens). **(general industry knowledge)**\n- **Carpets:** the main choice is broadloom vs carpet tile. Nylon is resilient and polypropylene is cheaper and stain-resistant. For commercial spaces, ask for fire classification and use class. **(general industry knowledge)**\n- **Wallpaper:** vinyl is washable and suits commercial use. In humid UAE interiors, wall moisture and mould-resistant adhesive matter. Commercial projects may also require fire classification. **(general industry knowledge)**\n\n## 7. What an agent should ask a buyer before quoting (checklist)\n\n1. Location and authority: DM, DDA, Trakhees/Nakheel, another free zone, or another emirate.\n2. Who obtains the NOC and permit: the buyer or the contractor (include this in scope and price).\n3. Is it commercial or public space (FR fabric and DCD-approved materials likely needed) or a private villa or apartment?\n4. Required certificates: DCL low-VOC, ECAS, FR test reports, panel E-class.\n5. Wet-area specifications: MR MDF or plywood, porcelain, slip rating.\n\n## Unverified items in this file\n\n- The DM fit-out permit scope, documents and fees (no official DM page was fetched).\n- The Dubai Contractor Register and grading under \"Law No. 7 of 2025\".\n- DDA minor-works exemption for internal painting.\n- The full list of Trakhees communities, and the Nakheel-to-Trakhees process.\n- UAE Fire and Life Safety Code test classes for curtains, upholstery and carpets, and the scope of the \"Emirates Safety Laboratory\" requirement.\n- Whether Al Sa'fat VOC figures are current (taken from 2017–2021 DCL certificates).\n- Whether Cabinet Resolution No. 6 of 2016 (paints) is current.\n- Any UAE formaldehyde E-class requirement for wood panels (none found).\n<<<END>>>\n\n<<<FILE knowledge/brief/uae_regulations/services_and_general_trades.md>>>\n# UAE regulations: services and general trades\n\n- **Scope (category codes):** cleaning, facade-cleaning, pest, movers, landscaping, job-shop-manufacturing, general-services-other\n- Compiled 2026-10-12 by Claude (it_engineer session) for the sales_engineer trade digest.\n- **Agents: cite this as general guidance; the authority's current text governs.**\n- Every source below was accessed 2026-10-12. \"(unverified)\" means the item came from a secondary source or could not be checked against an official text. Do not present it as a rule.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Cross-trade rules (all categories)\n\n### 1.1 The trade licence activity must match the work\n- A supplier's trade licence lists its permitted activities. The work quoted should fall within those activities. Each activity type maps to a specific trade activity code and may need separate Dubai Municipality approvals depending on scope (unverified, secondary source). Source: https://www.dubaisouthbh.com/blogs/building-cleaning-license-dubai\n- Licensing authorities: Dubai = Department of Economy and Tourism (DET, formerly DED); Abu Dhabi = Abu Dhabi Department of Economic Development (ADDED); Sharjah = Sharjah Economic Development Department (SEDD). Free-zone companies are licensed by their free-zone authority. (Authority names are general knowledge. The per-activity code lists were not fetched in this session.) (unverified)\n- Free-zone and consultancy guides give different activity codes for the same trade. For example, pest control appears as 749301 in one guide and 8129.92 in another. **Never quote an activity code.** Ask the supplier for its licence and read the activity text. Source: https://www.meydanfz.ae/activity-hub/apply-for-a-public-health-pests-control-services-license-in-dubai\n\n### 1.2 Midday work ban (MoHRE)\n- **Rule:** MoHRE bans work under direct sunlight and in open-air areas between **12:30 and 15:00, from 15 June to 15 September**. The 2025 edition was the 21st consecutive year. Source: https://mohre.gov.ae/en/media-center/news/3/6/2025/mohre-to-implement-midday-break-for-the-21st-consecutive-year-from-15-june-to-15-september-2025\n- **2026 dates:** no official 2026 notice was found. The same window is likely but unverified.\n- **Employer duties:** provide a shaded rest area, plus cooling, drinking water and first aid (secondary source). Daily hours during the ban period may not exceed 8 (secondary source).\n- **Exemptions:** work that technically must continue, such as asphalt or concrete pours and emergency repairs to utilities (secondary source).\n- **Fine:** AED 5,000 per worker, up to AED 50,000 where several workers are involved (secondary source). Source: https://gulfnews.com/uae/government/uae-announces-midday-break-for-outdoor-workers-from-june-15-to-sept-15-1.96142042\n- **Legal basis:** reported as Ministerial Resolution No. 44 of 2022 (Occupational Health and Safety and Labour Accommodation) (unverified, secondary source).\n- **Effect on quotes:** outdoor jobs between June and September (landscaping, facade work, moving, outdoor pest treatment) lose midday hours. Expect longer schedules or early and late shifts.\n\n### 1.3 VAT on quotes (FTA)\n- **Rate:** standard rate 5%, in force since 1 January 2018. Source: https://mof.gov.ae/?p=44399\n- **Mandatory registration:** required when taxable supplies and imports exceed **AED 375,000**. Source: https://mof.gov.ae/?p=44399\n- **Voluntary registration:** possible from AED 187,500 (secondary source; confirm on https://tax.gov.ae). Source: https://www.kayrouzandassociates.com/insights/vat-registration-uae-thresholds-fta-process-2026\n- **Buyer check:**\n  - A supplier that charges VAT must show its TRN (Tax Registration Number) and state the price both excluding and including VAT.\n  - A supplier below the threshold may lawfully charge no VAT. \"No VAT\" is not by itself a red flag.\n  - The TRN-on-invoice requirement is general knowledge and was not fetched this session (unverified).\n\n### 1.4 Consumer Protection (applies to consumers, not necessarily B2B)\n- **Law:** Federal Law No. 15 of 2020 on Consumer Protection replaced Federal Law No. 24 of 2006. It covers goods and services across the UAE, free zones included. Its Executive Regulation is Cabinet Decision No. 66 of 2023, in effect from 14 October 2023.\n  - Ministry of Economy legislation page: https://www.moet.gov.ae/en/consumer-protection-legislations\n  - Law-firm summary: https://www.pinsentmasons.com/en-gb/out-law/analysis/uae-consumer-protection-regime-revamped\n- **Supplier duties under the Ministry's framework:**\n  - announce prices\n  - honour warranties\n  - repair or re-provide a defective service at no charge\n  - provide contracts and invoices in Arabic (other languages may accompany them)\n  - protect consumer data\n- **Fine levels:** secondary sources cite fines of up to AED 100,000 for some violations (unverified). Source: https://mondaq.com/consumer-law/1495004/uae-implements-revised-penalties-and-amendments-under-consumer-protection-law\n- **Scope for QuotesDone:** whether the law protects a business buyer in a B2B deal is not established here (unverified). Do not tell a business buyer it is protected by this law.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Cleaning (`cleaning`)\n- **Licence:** a trade licence with a cleaning-services activity. The activity covering building or exterior cleaning is a separate activity from general cleaning (secondary source). Source: https://www.dubaisouthbh.com/blogs/building-cleaning-license-dubai\n- **Midday ban:** applies to any outdoor portion of the work (see 1.2).\n- **Chemicals:** a specific Dubai Municipality rule on cleaning chemicals was not found this session (unverified).\n- **Buyer should check:**\n  - the licence activity covers the exact service (office, residential, deep clean, kitchen exhaust and so on)\n  - staff count and shifts\n  - whether consumables and chemicals are included\n  - VAT treatment\n  - liability insurance (insurance is common practice, not a verified legal requirement)\n\n## 3. Facade cleaning: rope access and BMU (`facade-cleaning`)\n- **Dubai:** Dubai Municipality guideline DM-HSD-GU35 (\"Technical Guidelines for Usage of Rope Access System\", V3) says rope access should be done only by specialist, certified companies and personnel. It also says companies are checked on both technical and quality-assurance aspects. Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/DM-HSD-GU35-URAS2_Technical-Guidelines-for-Usage-of-Rope-Access-System_V3.pdf\n  - The PDF could not be machine-read in full this session. Its detailed thresholds (wind limits, anchor tests) are not reproduced here.\n- **IRATA:**\n  - Secondary sources say only IRATA-audited companies may run rope access in Dubai since October 2019, with technicians holding accredited certification (unverified). Source: https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/facade-cleaning-company-setup-dubai\n  - Rope operatives on high-rise structures need IRATA or an equivalent qualification (unverified). Source: https://www.meydanfz.ae/activity-hub/start-an-exterior-building-cleaning-business-in-dubai\n- **Sharjah:** the Sharjah Private Sector Safety Authority (SPSA) code OSHJ-CoP-04 \"Working at Height Safety\" (V1, Sept 2021) has a rope access section. Source: https://mc.spsa.shj.ae/Media/documents/0n5TzmzMWEWSovRZUMdXaA_4.%20Working%20at%20Height%20Safety%20Version%201%20English.pdf\n- **Abu Dhabi:** an OSHAD working-at-height code was not located this session (unverified). The international reference is ISO 22846-2:2012 on rope access: https://www.iso.org/standard/51354.html\n- **BMU (building maintenance unit, the gondola or cradle on the roof):** a BMU-specific authority approval process was not found (unverified). Ask for the building's BMU inspection or certification records, held by the building owner or facility manager.\n- **Road occupation:** RTA road-occupation permits are reported to be needed only when a cradle base or vehicle stands on a public road (unverified, secondary source).\n- **Buyer should check:**\n  - IRATA (or equivalent) company and technician certificates and their levels\n  - the method statement and risk assessment\n  - who provides anchors or the BMU\n  - third-party liability insurance\n  - weather stand-down terms\n  - midday-ban scheduling in summer\n\n## 4. Pest control (`pest`)\n- **Dubai NOC:**\n  - A company working in Dubai needs a No Objection Certificate from the **Dubai Municipality Public Health Pest Control Section** before it can get the trade licence.\n  - DM publishes a monthly list of authorised companies (Arabic). The listed companies are approved for **general pest control only**. Fumigation, termite control and bird control are excluded from that list.\n  - Companies are grouped by number of qualified staff: Group A (more than 25), Group B (11 to 25), Group C (1 to 10). DM rates performance from A (best) to E.\n  - Authorised list, July 2026: https://dmpmedia.dm.gov.ae/uploads/2023/06/قائمة-شركات-مكافحة-الحشرات-المصرح-لها-بالعمل-يوليو2026.pdf\n- **Pesticides:**\n  - Pesticides must be registered federally. The Dubai Municipality page links to the MoCCAE (Ministry of Climate Change and Environment) registered-pesticide directory. Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/?p=7618\n  - Registered companies must supply DM's Pest Control Section with the list of chemicals they use (secondary source, Gulf News). Source: https://gulfnews.com/uae/health/uae-pesticide-tragedy-clear-laws-in-place-1.1209226\n- **Staffing:** a qualified supervisor and operator are required, along with secure pesticide storage. Sources disagree on the supervisor's qualification (a degree vs a DM-approved certificate), so treat it as unverified.\n- **Residential fumigation:** fumigation and high-toxicity treatment have been tightly restricted after fatal incidents (Gulf News source above). Exact current rules are unverified.\n- **Buyer should check:**\n  - the company appears on the current DM authorised list (Dubai)\n  - the licence covers the specific service (general, termite or fumigation)\n  - product names are registered (MoCCAE)\n  - re-entry time and safety instructions\n  - the warranty or re-treatment period\n\n## 5. Movers (`movers`)\n- **Licence:** a trade licence with a moving / packing / transport activity. A mover-specific authority rule was not found this session (unverified).\n- **Vehicles:** goods vehicles fall under RTA (Dubai) or emirate transport-authority rules. Specific permit names are unverified.\n- **Building permissions:** many towers require the community or building management's move-in/move-out permit. This is building practice, not law (unverified).\n- **Midday ban:** applies to outdoor loading in summer (see 1.2).\n- **Buyer should check:**\n  - whether the price is fixed or by volume or truck\n  - packing materials\n  - insurance cover (goods in transit) and its limits\n  - dismantling and assembly\n  - access permits and who obtains them\n  - VAT\n\n## 6. Landscaping and irrigation (`landscaping`)\n- **Dubai TSE framework:** Dubai Municipality's legislation index lists these instruments. Their full text was not retrieved, and no clause numbers are cited.\n  - Local Order No. 8 of 2002 on sewage, irrigation and drainage\n  - Local Order No. 1 of 2015 on the use of treated sewage effluent (TSE)\n  - Resolution No. 21 of 2012 on fees for TSE use in private farm irrigation\n  - Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/?p=359668\n- **Recycled-water network:** Dubai irrigates green spaces with reclaimed water through a network of about 2,400 km. Source: https://www.dm.gov.ae/municipality-business/waste-sewerage-agency/\n- **Contractor registration:** Dubai Law No. 7 of 2025 on contracting activities (reported in effect from 8 January 2026) makes landscaping a classified contracting activity registered with DM, graded by certified staff (unverified, secondary source). Source: https://www.businessdubai.ae/blogs/landscaping-company-setup-dubai\n- **Design expectations:** DM is reported to check TSE (not potable) irrigation, drip efficiency, backflow prevention and salt-tolerant planting (unverified, same source).\n- **Midday ban:** applies (see 1.2).\n- **Buyer should check:**\n  - the water source (TSE or potable) and who arranges the connection (DM / DEWA)\n  - backflow prevention\n  - plant species list\n  - irrigation-controller spec\n  - plant-replacement warranty\n  - maintenance schedule\n  - the contractor's DM registration or grade (Dubai, 2026 onward)\n\n## 7. Job-shop manufacturing (`job-shop-manufacturing`)\n- No specific authority rule was located this session (unverified).\n- **Licence:** an industrial or workshop activity matching the process (fabrication, machining and so on). Industrial licensing and workshop location rules vary by emirate (unverified).\n- **Buyer should check:**\n  - the licence activity\n  - the material specification and grade, and mill certificates\n  - tolerances\n  - quantities\n  - lead time\n  - delivery terms\n  - inspection and acceptance\n  - VAT\n\n## 8. General services, other (`general-services-other`)\n- Apply section 1: matching licence activity, midday ban for outdoor work, VAT.\n- If the service touches a regulated trade (pest, rope access, electrical, gas), apply that trade's rules.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Glossary\n- **DET / ADDED / SEDD:** the economic licensing authorities of Dubai, Abu Dhabi and Sharjah.\n- **DM:** Dubai Municipality.\n- **MoCCAE:** Ministry of Climate Change and Environment (federal pesticide registration).\n- **MoHRE:** Ministry of Human Resources and Emiratisation.\n- **FTA:** Federal Tax Authority (VAT).\n- **TRN:** Tax Registration Number.\n- **NOC:** No Objection Certificate.\n- **IRATA:** Industrial Rope Access Trade Association.\n- **BMU:** building maintenance unit, the permanent roof-mounted cradle system.\n- **TSE:** treated sewage effluent, the recycled water used for irrigation.\n- **OSHAD:** Abu Dhabi Occupational Safety and Health Centre.\n- **SPSA:** Sharjah Private Sector Safety Authority.\n\n## 10. Quick buyer checklist (any quote)\n1. The supplier's licence activity matches the work, and the licence is valid in the emirate where the work happens.\n2. Trade-specific approval is present: DM pest list or NOC, IRATA for rope access, DM contractor registration for landscaping in Dubai.\n3. The price states whether VAT is included and shows a TRN if VAT is charged.\n4. Scope, exclusions, materials and chemicals are named, along with the warranty or re-service terms.\n5. Insurance (liability, goods in transit) is stated where relevant.\n6. Summer outdoor work allows for the 12:30–15:00 midday ban (15 June to 15 September).\n<<<END>>>";
